hadiths[317]{hadithnumber,text}:
"82","Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abu Ishaq, from Haritha, he said: People of Sham came to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: We have acquired wealth, horses, and slaves; we wish there to be therein alms-tax and purification for us. He said: What my two companions did before me, I will do. He consulted the companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), among them Ali, may Allah be pleased with him. Ali said: It is good, if it is not a fixed tribute taken from them after you."
"83","Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Abu Wail, that al-Subayy ibn Mabad was a Christian, Taghlibi, Bedouin, and became Muslim. He asked which deed is best, and it was said to him: Striving in the path of Allah, mighty and majestic. He wanted to strive, and it was said to him: Have you performed pilgrimage? He said: No. It was said: Perform pilgrimage and minor pilgrimage, then strive. He set out until, when he was at al-Hawabit, he entered ihram for both together. Zayd ibn Suhan and Salman ibn Rabia saw him and said: He is more astray than his camel, or: he is no better guided than his she-camel. He went to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and informed him of their saying. He said: You were guided to the practice of your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Al-Hakam said: I said to Abu Wail: Did al-Subayy narrate to you? He said: Yes."
"84","Affan narrated to us, Shuba narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, he said: I heard Amr ibn Maymun say: Umar led us in the dawn prayer at Jam, then stood and said: The polytheists used not to depart until the sun rose, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) opposed them, then departed before the sun rose."
"85","Affan narrated to us, Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Asim ibn Kulayb narrated to us, he said: My father said: Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, narrated it to us, he said: What pleases you of that? Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, when he called the elders of the companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), called me with them and said: Do not speak until they speak. He said: So he called us one day or one night and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said concerning the Night of Decree what you know; so seek it in the last ten, odd. In which odd do you judge it?"
"86","Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, Shuba narrated to us, he said: I heard Asim ibn Amr al-Bajali narrate from a man of the people who asked Umar ibn al-Khattab, and they said to him: We came to you only to ask you about three: about a man's voluntary prayer in his house, and about washing from major impurity, and about a man, what is lawful for him of his wife when she is menstruating. He said: Are you sorcerers? You asked me about something none asked me since I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about it. He said: A man's voluntary prayer in his house is light; whoever wishes, let him light his house. He said concerning washing from major impurity: he washes his private part, then performs ablution, then pours over his head three times. He said concerning the menstruating woman: for him is what is above the waist-wrapper."
"87","Qutayba ibn Said narrated to us, Ibn Lahia narrated to us, from Abu al-Nadr, from Abu Salama, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: I saw Sad ibn Abi Waqqas wipe over his leather socks in Iraq when performing ablution, and I disapproved of that of him. He said: When we gathered with Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, he said to me: Ask your father about what you disapproved of me of wiping over the socks. He said: So I mentioned that to him, and he said: When Sad narrates something to you, do not reject it of him, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to wipe over the leather socks."
"88","Harun ibn Maruf narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Abu al-Nadr, from Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abdullah ibn Umar, from Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he wiped over the leather socks, and that Abdullah ibn Umar asked Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, about that, and he said: Yes, when Sad narrates to you something from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), do not ask another than him about it."
"89","Affan narrated to us, Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Qatada narrated to us, from Salim ibn Abi al-Jad al-Ghatafani, from Madan ibn Abi Talha al-Yamari, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, stood upon the minbar on Friday, praised Allah and extolled Him, then mentioned the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, then said: I saw a dream I judge only for the nearness of my term: I saw as if a rooster pecked me twice. He said: And it was mentioned to me that it is a red rooster. I narrated it to Asma daughter of Umays, wife of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with them, and she said: A man of the foreigners will kill you. He said: People command me to appoint a successor, and Allah would not neglect His religion and His caliphate with which He sent His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). If my affair hastens, then consultation is among these six with whom the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died while pleased with them; whomever of them you pledge, listen to him and obey. I know people will criticize this affair; I fought them with this hand of mine for Islam; those are the enemies of Allah, the disbelievers, the misguided. By Allah, I leave nothing in what my Lord entrusted to me, and He made me successor, more important to me than the kalala. By Allah, the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was not sterner with me in anything since I accompanied him than he was stern with me in the affair of the kalala, until he struck my chest with his finger and said: The summer verse revealed at the end of the sura of Women suffices you. If I live, I will judge therein with a judgment known by whoever reads and whoever does not read. I call Allah to witness over the commanders of the garrisons: I sent them only to teach people their religion and clarify for them the practice of their Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and raise to me what is obscure to them. Then you, O people, eat of two trees I judge only foul: this garlic and onion. By Allah, I used to see the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) find their smell from a man and order him taken by his hand and brought out of the mosque until brought to Baqi. Whoever eats them must kill them by cooking. He said: So he addressed the people on Friday and was struck on Wednesday."
"90","Yaqub narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, he said: Nafi, freedman of Abdullah ibn Umar, narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Umar, he said: I went out with al-Zubayr and al-Miqdad ibn al-Aswad to our properties in Khaybar to inspect them. When we arrived, we scattered in our properties. He said: I was attacked at night while sleeping on my bed, and my hands were dislocated from the elbows. In the morning my two companions were called for me, and they came to me and asked me who did this to you; I said: I do not know. He said: So they set my hands, then brought me to Umar. He said: This is the work of the Jews. Then he stood among the people as an addresser and said: O people, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dealt with the Jews of Khaybar upon that we expel them when we wish, and they attacked Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, and dislocated his hands as reached you, with their attack upon the Ansar before him; we do not doubt they are their people; we have no enemy there but them. Whoever has property in Khaybar, let him join it, for I am expelling the Jews. So he expelled them."
"91","Hasan ibn Musa and Husayn ibn Muhammad narrated to us, they said: Shayban narrated to us, from Yahya, from Abu Salama, from Abu Hurayra, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, while addressing on Friday, a man came, and Umar said: Why do you delay from prayer? The man said: It was only that I heard the call and performed ablution. He said: Also, did you not hear that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) says: When one of you goes to Friday prayer, let him bathe."
"92","Hasan ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, he said: Asim al-Ahwal narrated to us, from Abu Uthman, he said: The letter of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, came to us while we were in Azerbaijan: O Utba ibn Farqad, beware of luxury and the dress of the people of polytheism and wearing silk, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us wearing silk and said: except like this, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raised for us his two fingers."
"93","Hasan narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahia narrated to us, Abu al-Aswad narrated to us, that he heard Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Labiba narrate from Abu Sinan al-Duali that he entered upon Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, with a group of the first emigrants with him. Umar sent to a coffer brought from a fortress of Iraq, and therein was a ring. A son of his took it and put it in his mouth, and Umar pulled it from him, then Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, wept. Those with him said to him: Why do you weep, when Allah has opened for you and made you prevail over your enemy and cooled your eye? Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: This world is not opened upon anyone except that Allah, mighty and majestic, casts between them enmity and hatred until the Day of Resurrection, and I fear that."
"94","Yaqub narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, Nafi narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Umar, from his father, he said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): How does one of us act when he is in major impurity then wishes to sleep before bathing? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Let him perform ablution as his ablution for prayer, then let him sleep."
"95","Yaqub narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, al-Zuhri narrated to me, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba ibn Masud, from Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, he said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, say: When Abdullah ibn Ubayy died, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was called to pray over him. He stood to him, and when he stood over him intending prayer, I moved until I stood at his chest and said: O Messenger of Allah, over the enemy of Allah, Abdullah ibn Ubayy, who said on such-and-such day such-and-such, counting his days. He said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was smiling, until, when I pressed much upon him, he said: Step back from me, O Umar; I was given choice and chose, and it was said: Ask forgiveness for them or do not ask forgiveness for them; if you ask forgiveness for them seventy times, Allah will not forgive them. Had I known, had I exceeded seventy, he would be forgiven, I would have exceeded. He said: Then he prayed over him and walked with him and stood at his grave until he was finished. He said: Wonder at me and my boldness toward the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: By Allah, it was only a little until these two verses were revealed: And never pray over any of them who dies, ever, nor stand at his grave; they disbelieved in Allah and His Messenger and died while wicked. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never prayed after him over a hypocrite, nor stood at his grave, until Allah, mighty and majestic, took him."
"96","Yaqub narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, as he narrated to me from him, Nafi his freedman, he said: Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, used to say: When a man has only one garment, let him wrap his waist with it, then let him pray, for I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, say that, and say: Do not wrap with the garment when it is alone as the Jews do. Nafi said: Had I told you he traced it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I would hope I did not lie."
"97","Muammal narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us, he said: Ziyad ibn Mikhraq narrated to us, from Shahr, from Uqba ibn Amir, he said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever dies believing in Allah and the Last Day, it is said to him: Enter Paradise from whichever of the eight gates of Paradise you wish."
"98","Aswad ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Jafar, meaning al-Ahmar, informed us, from Mutarrif, from al-Hakam, from Mujahid, he said: A man struck his son with a sword and killed him, and he was brought to Umar. He said: Had I not heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: No father is retaliated against for his son, I would have killed you before you left."
"99","Aswad ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Amash, Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abis ibn Rabia, he said: I saw Umar look at the Stone and say: By Allah, had I not seen the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kiss you, I would not kiss you. Then he kissed it."
"100","Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shuayb informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: al-Saib ibn Yazid, son of the sister of Namir, informed us that Huwaytib ibn Abd al-Uzza informed him that Abdullah ibn al-Sadi informed him that he came to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, in his caliphate, and Umar said to him: Was I not informed that you undertake works of the people, and when given the wage you dislike it? He said: I said: Yes. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Then what do you want with that? He said: I said: I have horses and slaves, and I am well, and I want my wage to be charity upon the Muslims. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Do not do it, for I wanted what you wanted, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to give me gifts, and I would say: Give it to one poorer to it than me, until he once gave me wealth, and I said: Give it to one poorer to it than me. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Take it and keep it as wealth and give it in charity; whatever comes to you of this wealth while you are not aspiring nor asking, take it, and what not, do not follow it with yourself."
"101","Sakan ibn Nafi al-Bahili narrated to us, he said: Salih narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Rabi'ah ibn Darraj narrated to me that Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, prayed two rak'ahs after Asr on the road to Makkah, and Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, saw him and became angry with him, then he said: By Allah, indeed you know that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade them."
"102","Muhammad ibn Yazid narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, from a man of Quraysh of Banu Sahm, from a man of them called Majidah, he said: I quarreled with a boy in Makkah and he bit my ear and cut off part of it, or I bit his ear and cut off part of it, and when Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, came to us performing Hajj, we were brought before him, and he said: Take them both to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and if the offender has reached the age at which retaliation can be taken from him, then let retaliation be taken. He said: When we were brought to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, he looked at us and said: Yes, this one has reached the age at which retaliation can be taken from him; call a cupper for me. When he mentioned the cupper, he said: Indeed I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: I have given my maternal aunt a boy, and I hope that Allah will bless her in him, and I have forbidden her to make him a cupper, or a butcher, or a goldsmith."
"103","Ya'qub narrated to us: my father narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, he said: and al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, from a man of Banu Sahm, from Ibn Majidah al-Sahmi, that he said: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, performed Hajj over us during his caliphate, then he mentioned the hadith."
"104","Ubaydah ibn Humayd narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Sa'id, he said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, addressed the people and said: Indeed Allah, Mighty and Majestic, granted a concession to His Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, as He willed, and indeed the Prophet of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, has passed on his way, so complete Hajj and Umrah as Allah, Mighty and Majestic, commanded you, and guard the chastity of these women."
"105","Ubaydah ibn Humayd narrated to us: Ubayd Allah ibn Umar narrated to me, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was asked: Does a man sleep when he is junub? He said: Yes, when he performs ablution."
"106","al-Hasan ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak informed us, he said: Ma'mar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Rabi'ah ibn Darraj, that Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, prayed two rak'ahs after Asr, and Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, became angry with him and said: Do you not know that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to forbid us from them."
"107","Abu al-Mughirah narrated to us: Safwan narrated to us: Shurayh ibn Ubayd narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I went out seeking the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, before I became Muslim, and I found that he had preceded me to the mosque, so I stood behind him, and he began with Surah al-Haqqah, and I began to admire the composition of the Quran. He said: And I said: By Allah, this is a poet, as Quraysh said. He said: Then he recited: Indeed it is the word of a noble Messenger, and it is not the word of a poet, little do you believe. He said: I said: A soothsayer. He said: Nor the word of a soothsayer, little do you remember. A revelation from the Lord of the worlds. And had he fabricated against Us some sayings, We would have seized him by the right hand, then cut from him the aorta, and none of you could have shielded him, to the end of the surah. He said: So Islam fell into my heart at every place."
"108","Abu al-Mughirah and Isam ibn Khalid said: Safwan narrated to us, from Shurayh ibn Ubayd and Rashid ibn Sa'd and others, they said: When Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, reached Sargh, he was told that there was a severe plague in Sham. He said: It has reached me that the severity of the plague is in Sham, and I said: If my term comes while Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah is alive, I will appoint him as successor, for if Allah asks me why I appointed him over the ummah of Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, I will say: Indeed I heard Your Messenger, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: Every prophet has a trustee, and my trustee is Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah. But the people disapproved of that and said: What is the matter with the elite of Quraysh, meaning Banu Fihr. Then he said: And if my term comes while Abu Ubaydah has died, I will appoint Mu'adh ibn Jabal, for if my Lord, Mighty and Majestic, asks me why I appointed him, I will say: I heard Your Messenger, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: Indeed he will be resurrected on the Day of Resurrection ahead of the scholars by a rank."
"109","Abu al-Mughirah narrated to us: Ibn Ayyash narrated to us, he said: al-Awza'i and others narrated to me, from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: A boy was born to the brother of Umm Salamah, the wife of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and they named him al-Walid, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: You have named him with the names of your Pharaohs; there will indeed be in this ummah a man called al-Walid who will be more evil to this ummah than Pharaoh was to his people."
"110","Bahz narrated to us: Aban narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Aliyah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Trustworthy men testified to me, among them Umar, and the most pleasing of them to me was Umar, that the Prophet of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to say: There is no prayer after the Asr prayer until the sun sets, and no prayer after the Fajr prayer until the sun rises."
"111","Abu al-Mughirah narrated to us: Safwan narrated to us: Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr narrated to us, from al-Harith ibn Mu'awiyah al-Kindi, that he rode to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, to ask him about three matters. He said: So he came to Madinah, and Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, asked him: What brought you? He said: To ask you about three matters. He said: And what are they? He said: Sometimes I and my wife are in a narrow structure and prayer comes; if I pray with her she will be beside me, and if she prays behind me she will go out of the structure. So Umar said: Screen between you and her with a garment, then pray beside her if you wish. And about the two rak'ahs after Asr, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade me from them. He said: And about storytelling, for they urged me to tell stories. So he said: As you wish, as though he disliked to forbid him. He said: I only intended to stop at your word. He said: I fear for you that you will tell stories and become exalted over them in yourself, then you will tell stories and become exalted until it seems to you that you are above them like the Pleiades, and Allah will place you beneath their feet on the Day of Resurrection to that extent."
"112","Bishr ibn Shu'ayb ibn Abi Hamzah narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to me, from al-Zuhri, he said: Salim ibn Abd Allah informed me that Abd Allah ibn Umar informed him that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: Indeed Allah, Mighty and Majestic, forbids you to swear by your fathers. Umar said: By Allah, I have not sworn by them since I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbid them, nor have I uttered them deliberately or narrating them."
"113","Abu al-Yaman narrated to us: Abu Bakr ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, from Rashid ibn Sa'd, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, did not take sadaqah from horses and slaves."
"114","Ali ibn Ishaq narrated to us: Abd Allah, meaning Ibn al-Mubarak, informed us: Muhammad ibn Suqah informed us, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, addressed the people at al-Jabiyah and said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, stood among us like my standing among you, and said: Treat my companions well, then those who follow them, then those who follow them, then lying will spread until a man will begin with testimony before he is asked for it. So whoever of you desires the center of Paradise, let him hold to the community, for Satan is with the one, and he is farther from the two. Let none of you be alone with a woman, for Satan is the third of them. And whoever is pleased by his good deed and grieved by his bad deed, he is a believer."
"115","Abu al-Yaman narrated to us: Abu Bakr narrated to us, from Hakim ibn Umayr and Damrah ibn Habib, they said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Whoever is pleased to look at the guidance of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, let him look at the guidance of Amr ibn al-Aswad."
"116","Abu Sa'id, the freedman of Banu Hashim, narrated to us, he said: Za'idah narrated to us: Simak narrated to us, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Umar said: We were with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in a party, and a man said: No, by my father. So a man said: Do not swear by your fathers. So I turned, and behold, it was the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"117","Isam ibn Khalid and Abu al-Yaman said: Shu'ayb ibn Abi Hamzah informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utbah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us that Abu Hurayrah said: When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, died, and Abu Bakr was after him, and those of the Arabs who disbelieved disbelieved, Umar said: O Abu Bakr, how will you fight the people when the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: I have been commanded to fight the people until they say: There is no god but Allah; so whoever says: There is no god but Allah, has protected from me his wealth and his self except by its right, and his reckoning is upon Allah, exalted is He. Abu Bakr said: By Allah, I will surely fight whoever separates prayer and zakah, for zakah is the right of wealth; by Allah, had they withheld from me a young goat they used to render to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, I would have fought them for withholding it. Umar said: By Allah, it was only that I saw that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, had expanded the chest of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, for fighting, and I knew that it was the truth."
"118","Abu al-Mughirah narrated to us: al-Awza'i narrated to us: Amr ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn al-As, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: There is no prayer after the Fajr prayer until the sun rises, and none after Asr until the sun sets."
"119","al-Hakam ibn Nafi narrated to us: Ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abu Saba Utbah ibn Tamim, from al-Walid ibn Amir al-Yazani, from Urwah ibn Mughith al-Ansari, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, judged that the owner of the mount is most entitled to its front."
"120","Abu al-Yaman al-Hakam ibn Nafi narrated to us: Abu Bakr ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, from Rashid ibn Sa'd, from Humrah ibn Abd Kulal, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, marched to Sham after his first march to it, until when he overlooked it, it reached him and those with him that the plague was widespread in it, so his companions said to him: Return and do not plunge into it, for had you entered it while it was in it, we would not approve your leaving it. So he turned back toward Madinah, and he camped that night, and I was the nearest of the people to him, and when he set out I set out with him behind him, and I heard him say: They turned me back from Sham after I had overlooked it because the plague is in it; yet my turning from it will not delay my term, and my entering it would not have hastened my term; and had I reached Madinah and finished needs I must do, I would have marched until I entered Sham, then settled in Hims, for indeed I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: Allah will surely raise from it on the Day of Resurrection seventy thousand with no reckoning upon them and no punishment upon them; their raising will be between the olives and its wall in the red barren land of it."
"121","Abd Allah ibn Yazid narrated to us: Haywah informed us: Abu Aqil informed us, from his cousin, from Uqbah ibn Amir, that he went out with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in the expedition of Tabuk, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sat one day narrating to his companions, and said: Whoever stands when the sun declines, then performs ablution and performs it well, then stands and prays two rak'ahs, his sins will be forgiven and he will be as his mother bore him. Uqbah ibn Amir said: So I said: Praise be to Allah who provided me to hear this from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. So Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, who was sitting facing me, said to me: Do you marvel at this? The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said something more marvelous than this before you came. So I said: And what is that, may my father and mother be for you? Umar said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever performs ablution and performs it well, then raises his gaze to the sky and says: I testify that there is no god but Allah alone with no partner for Him, and I testify that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger, the eight gates of Paradise will be opened for him; he may enter through whichever of them he wishes."
"122","Sulayman ibn Dawud, meaning Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi, narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Dawud al-Awdi, from Abd al-Rahman al-Musalli, from al-Ash'ath ibn Qays, he said: I was a guest of Umar, and he reached for his wife and struck her, and said: O Ash'ath, remember from me three things I remembered from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: Do not ask a man for what he struck his wife, and do not sleep except upon witr, and I forgot the third."
"123","Abd al-Samad narrated to us: my father narrated to us: Yazid, meaning al-Rishk, narrated to us, from Mu'adhah, from Umm Amr the daughter of Abd Allah, that she heard Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr say: She heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, say in his sermon: Indeed he heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: Whoever wears silk in this world will not be clothed with it in the Hereafter."
"124","Yahya ibn Ishaq narrated to us: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, informed me, he said: I heard the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: A rider will surely pass through the surroundings of Madinah, then he will say: There was once in this place a large presence of believers. My father Ahmad ibn Hanbal said: Hasan al-Ashyab did not go beyond Jabir."
"125","Harun narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us: Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to me that Umar ibn al-Sa'ib narrated to him that al-Qasim ibn Abi al-Qasim al-Saba'i narrated to him, from the storyteller of the troops in Constantinople, that he heard him narrate that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: O people, indeed I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him not sit at a table around which wine is passed, and whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him not enter the bathhouse except with a waist-wrapper, and whichever woman believes in Allah and the Last Day, let her not enter the bathhouse."
"126","Abu Salamah al-Khuza'i narrated to us: Layth informed us, and Yunus narrated to us: Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abd Allah ibn Usamah ibn al-Had, from al-Walid ibn Abi al-Walid, from Uthman ibn Abd Allah, meaning Ibn Suraqah, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: Whoever shades the head of a fighter, Allah will shade him on the Day of Resurrection, and whoever equips a fighter until he becomes independent has the like of his reward until he dies. He said: Yunus said: Or returns. And whoever builds for Allah a mosque in which the name of Allah, exalted is He, is mentioned, Allah will build for him thereby a house in Paradise."
"127","Affan narrated to us: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Sulayman al-A'mash, from Shaqiq, from Salman ibn Rabi'ah, he said: I heard Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, say: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, distributed a distribution, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, others than these are more deserving of it, the people of the Suffah. He said: So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Indeed you leave me a choice between your asking me rudely and your making me miserly, and I am not miserly."
"128","Affan narrated to us: Khalid narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Asim ibn Ubayd Allah, from his father, from his grandfather, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, after ritual impurity, perform ablution and wipe over the two leather socks."
"129","Affan narrated to us: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Abu Rafi, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, was leaning upon Ibn Abbas, and with him were Ibn Umar and Sa'id ibn Zayd, may Allah be pleased with them both, and he said: Know that I have said nothing about the kalalah, and I will not appoint anyone after me as successor, and that whoever lives to my death among the Arab captives is free from the wealth of Allah, Mighty and Majestic. So Sa'id ibn Zayd said: Yet had you indicated a man of the Muslims, the people would have trusted you, and Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, did that and the people trusted him. So Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Indeed I have seen bad greed among my companions, and I am placing this matter with these six men with whom the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, died while he was pleased with them. Then Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Had one of two men lived to me, then I placed this matter with him, I would have trusted him: Salim, the freedman of Abu Hudhayfah, and Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah."
"130","Affan narrated to us: Hammam narrated to us: Qatadah narrated to us: Abu al-Aliyah narrated to me, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Trustworthy men testified to me, among them Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and the most pleasing of them to me was Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: There is no prayer after Fajr until the sun rises, and no prayer after Asr until the sun sets."
"131","Affan narrated to us: Wuhayb narrated to us: Abd Allah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, bent over the Corner and said: Indeed I know that you are a stone, and had I not seen my beloved, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, kiss you and touch you, I would not have touched you nor kissed you, and indeed there was for you in the Messenger of Allah a good example."
"132","Affan narrated to us: Hammad narrated to us: Ammar ibn Abi Ammar informed us that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Indeed the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saw a gold ring on a man's hand and said: Throw this away. So he threw it away, then he wore a ring of iron, so he said: This is worse than it. So he wore a ring of silver, and he was silent about him."
"133","Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to us: Za'idah narrated to us: Asim narrated to us, and Husayn ibn Ali, from Za'idah, from Asim, from Zirr, from Abd Allah, he said: When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was taken, the Ansar said: From us an amir and from you an amir. So Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, came to them and said: O company of the Ansar, do you not know that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, commanded Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, to lead the people in prayer? So which of you would like to precede Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him? So the Ansar said: We seek refuge with Allah from preceding Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him."
"134","Musa ibn Dawud narrated to us: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, informed him that he saw a man perform ablution for prayer and leave the place of a fingernail on the back of his foot, and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saw him and said: Return and perfect your ablution. So he returned, performed ablution, then prayed."
"135","Abu Sa'id, the freedman of Banu Hashim, narrated to us: al-Haytham ibn Rafi al-Tatari, a Basran, narrated to us: Abu Yahya, a man of the people of Makkah, narrated to me, from Farrukh, the freedman of Uthman, that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, while he was then the Commander of the Believers, went out to the mosque and saw scattered food, and said: What is this food? So they said: Food brought to us. He said: May Allah bless it and whoever brought it. It was said: O Commander of the Believers, it has been hoarded. He said: And who hoarded it? They said: Farrukh, the freedman of Uthman, and so-and-so, the freedman of Umar. So he sent to them and called them, and said: What carried you to hoard the food of the Muslims? They said: O Commander of the Believers, we buy with our wealth and sell. So Umar said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: Whoever hoards the food of the Muslims, Allah will strike him with bankruptcy or leprosy. So Farrukh said then: O Commander of the Believers, I covenant with Allah and covenant with you never to return to food again. As for the freedman of Umar, he said: We only buy with our wealth and sell. Abu Yahya said: Indeed I saw the freedman of Umar afflicted with leprosy."
"136","Abu al-Yaman narrated to us: Shu'ayb informed us, from al-Zuhri: Salim ibn Abd Allah narrated to us that Abd Allah ibn Umar said: I heard Umar say: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to give me a gift, and I would say: Give it to one poorer than I, until he once gave me wealth, and I said: Give it to one poorer than I. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Take it, acquire it as property and give it in charity; whatever of this wealth comes to you while you are neither craving nor asking, take it, and whatever is otherwise, do not attach your soul to it."
"137","Harun narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from his father, he said: I heard Umar say: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to give me a gift, then he mentioned its meaning."
"138","Hajjaj narrated to us: Layth narrated to us: Bukayr narrated to me, from Abd al-Malik ibn Sa'id al-Ansari, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I felt desire one day and kissed while I was fasting, so I came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: Today I did a grave matter; I kissed while fasting. So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: What do you think, had you rinsed with water while fasting? I said: There is no harm in that. So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Then for what?"
"139","Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us: Dawud, meaning Ibn Abi al-Furat, narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Buraydah, from Abu al-Aswad, that he said: I came to Madinah and reached it while a sickness had fallen in it, and they were dying a swift death, and I sat with Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and a bier passed by him and its owner was praised with good, so Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: It has become due. Then another passed and its owner was praised with good, so Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: It has become due. Then a third passed and it was praised with evil, so Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: It has become due. So Abu al-Aswad said: What has become due, O Commander of the Believers? He said: I said as the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Any Muslim for whom four testify with good, Allah will admit him to Paradise. He said: So we said: And three? He said: So he said: And three. He said: We said: And two? He said: And two. He said: Then we did not ask him about one."
"140","Abu Sa'id narrated to us: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us: Bukayr narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab, from Umar, he said: We fought with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in Ramadan, and the Conquest was in Ramadan, and we broke fast in both."
"141","Abu Sa'id, the freedman of Banu Hashim, narrated to us: al-Muthanna ibn Awf al-Anazi, a Basran, narrated to us, he said: al-Ghadban ibn Hanzalah informed us that his father Hanzalah ibn Nu'aym came as a delegate to Umar, and when any man of the delegation passed by Umar he would ask him from whom he was, until my father passed by him and he asked him: From whom are you? So he said: From Anazah. So he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: A tribe from here, wronged yet aided."
"142","Hasan ibn Musa narrated to us: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Habib narrated to us, from Ma'mar, that he asked Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab about fasting while traveling, so he narrated to him, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: We fought with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in two expeditions in the month of Ramadan, the day of Badr and the day of the Conquest, and we broke fast in both."
"143","Abu Sa'id narrated to us: Daylam ibn Ghazwan, an Abdi, narrated to us: Maymun al-Kurdi narrated to us: Abu Uthman al-Nahdi narrated to me, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Indeed the most fearful of what I fear for my ummah is every hypocrite eloquent of tongue."
"144","Abu Sa'id narrated to us: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us: Salih ibn Muhammad ibn Za'idah narrated to us, from Salim ibn Abd Allah, that he was with Maslamah ibn Abd al-Malik in the land of the Romans, and booty-theft was found in a man's baggage, so he asked Salim ibn Abd Allah, so he said: Abd Allah narrated to me, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever you find booty-theft in his baggage, burn it. He said: And I think he said: And strike him. He said: So he brought out his baggage in the market. He said: And he found in it a mushaf, so he asked Salim, so he said: Sell it and give its price in charity."
"145","Abu Sa'id and Husayn ibn Muhammad said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Maymun, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to seek refuge from five: from miserliness, cowardice, the trial of the chest, the punishment of the grave, and the worst age."
"146","Abu Sa'id narrated to us: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, he said: I heard Ata ibn Dinar, from Abu Yazid al-Khawlani, that he heard Fadalah ibn Ubayd say: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, that he heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: The martyrs are three: a believing man, good in faith, who meets the enemy and is true to Allah until he is killed; that is the one to whom the people raise their necks on the Day of Resurrection, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, raised his head until his cap fell, or the cap of Umar; and a believing man, good in faith, who meets the enemy and it is as though his skin is struck with acacia thorns; a stray arrow comes to him and kills him; he is in the second rank; and a believing man, good in faith, who mixed a righteous deed and another evil one, meets the enemy and is true to Allah until he is killed; that is in the third rank."
"147","Abu Sa'id narrated to us: Abd Allah ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us: Amr ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: A father is not subjected to retaliation for a son. And the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Wealth is inherited by whoever inherits the wala."
"148","Hasan narrated to us: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us: Amr ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, from his father, from Abd Allah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: Retaliation is not taken for a son from his father."
"149","Hasan narrated to us: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us: al-Dahhak ibn Shurahbil narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, perform ablution once once."
"150","Yahya ibn Ishaq narrated to us: Ibn Lahi'ah informed us, from Ata ibn Dinar, from Abu Yazid al-Khawlani, he said: I heard Fadalah ibn Ubayd say: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, say: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: The martyrs are four: a believing man, good in faith, who meets the enemy and is true to Allah and is killed; that is the one whom the people look at thus, and he raised his head until the cap of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, fell, or the cap of Umar; and the second is a believing man who meets the enemy and it is as though his back is struck with acacia thorns; a stray arrow comes to him and kills him; that is in the second rank; and the third is a believing man who mixed a righteous deed and another evil one, meets the enemy and is true to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, until he is killed. He said: That is in the third rank. And the fourth is a believing man who was greatly excessive over himself, meets the enemy and is true to Allah until he is killed; that is in the fourth rank."
"151","Yahya ibn Ghaylan narrated to us: Rishdin ibn Sa'd narrated to us: Abu Abd Allah al-Ghafiqi narrated to me, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, that he performed ablution in the year of Tabuk once once."
"152","Hasan narrated to us: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jabir, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, informed him that he heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: The people of Makkah will surely leave, then none will pass by it or know it except a few, then it will fill and be built, then they will leave it and never return to it again."
"153","al-Hasan narrated to us: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jabir, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, informed him that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saw a man perform ablution for the Zuhr prayer and leave the place of a fingernail on the back of his foot, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saw him and said: Return and perfect your ablution. So he returned, performed ablution, then prayed."
"154","Hushaym narrated to us, he said: al-Zuhri claimed, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utbah ibn Mas'ud, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Do not extol me as the Christians extolled Isa son of Maryam, peace upon him, for I am only the servant of Allah and His Messenger."
"155","Hushaym narrated to us: Abu Bishr informed us, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, he said: This verse was revealed while the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was hidden in Makkah: And do not recite loudly in your prayer nor be silent in it. He said: When he prayed with his companions he raised his voice with the Quran. He said: When the polytheists heard that, they reviled the Quran and Him who sent it and him who brought it. So Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said to His Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: And do not recite loudly in your prayer, meaning your recitation, lest the polytheists hear and revile the Quran, nor be silent in it from your companions so as not to make them hear the Quran until they take it from you, but seek between that a way."
"156","Hushaym narrated to us: Ali ibn Zayd informed us, from Yusuf ibn Mihran, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, addressed, and Hushaym once said: He addressed us, and he praised Allah, exalted is He, and lauded Him, then he mentioned stoning and said: Do not be deceived about it, for indeed it is a hadd of the huddud of Allah, exalted is He. Indeed the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, stoned, and we stoned after him, and were it not that men would say: Umar added to the Book of Allah what is not of it, I would have written it in a margin of the mushaf. Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, testified, and Hushaym once said: And Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf and so-and-so and so-and-so, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, stoned, and we stoned after him. Indeed there will be after you people who will deny stoning, and the Dajjal, and intercession, and the punishment of the grave, and people brought out of the Fire after they were charred."
"157","Hushaym narrated to us: Humayd informed us, from Anas, he said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I agreed with my Lord in three: I said: O Messenger of Allah, had we taken from the Maqam of Ibrahim a prayer place, then there was revealed: And take from the Maqam of Ibrahim a prayer place. And I said: O Messenger of Allah, indeed your wives are visited by the righteous and the wicked, so had you commanded them to veil, then the verse of the veil was revealed. And the wives of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, united in jealousy, so I said to them: Perhaps his Lord, if he divorces you, will replace for him wives better than you. He said: So it was revealed likewise."
"158","Abd al-A'la ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard Hisham ibn Hakim ibn Hizam recite Surah al-Furqan, and he recited in it letters the Prophet of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, had not taught me. He said: So I wanted to assault him while he was in prayer, but when he finished I said: Who taught you this recitation? He said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. I said: You lie; by Allah, it is not thus that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, taught you. So I took his hand leading him, and set out with him to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: O Messenger of Allah, indeed you taught me Surah al-Furqan, and indeed I heard this one recite in it letters you had not taught me. So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Recite, O Hisham. So he recited as he had recited. So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Thus it was sent down. Then he said: Recite, O Umar. So I recited, and he said: Thus it was sent down. Then the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Indeed the Quran was sent down in seven ahruf."
"159","Amr ibn al-Haytham narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, from Umar, he said: Indeed I saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, writhing, finding nothing with which to fill his stomach from poor dates."
"160","Ibn Abi Adi narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas, he said: Umar said: I agreed with my Lord, Mighty and Majestic, in three, or my Lord agreed with me in three. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, had you taken the Maqam as a prayer place. He said: So Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent down: And take from the Maqam of Ibrahim a prayer place. And I said: Had you veiled the Mothers of the Believers, for the righteous and the wicked visit you, so the verse of the veil was sent down. He said: And something reached me about the Mothers of the Believers, so I went through them saying to them: You shall surely desist from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, or Allah will surely replace for him wives better than you, Muslim, until I came to one of the Mothers of the Believers and she said: O Umar, is there not in the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, what admonishes his wives until you admonish them? So I desisted, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent down: Perhaps his Lord, if he divorces you, will replace for him wives better than you, Muslim, believing, devout, the verse."
"161","al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us: al-Awza'i narrated to us that Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to him, from Ikrimah, the freedman of Ibn Abbas, he said: I heard Ibn Abbas say: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, say: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, while he was in al-Aqiq, say: Tonight a comer from my Lord came to me and said: Pray in this blessed valley and say: Umrah in Hajj. al-Walid said: Meaning Dhu al-Hulayfah."
"162","Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri: He heard Malik ibn Aws ibn al-Hadathan; he heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, say: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said, and Sufyan once said: He heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: Gold for silver is riba except hand to hand, and wheat for wheat is riba except hand to hand, and barley for barley is riba except hand to hand, and dates for dates are riba except hand to hand."
"163","Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri: He heard Abu Ubayd, he said: I witnessed the Eid with Umar, and he began with the prayer before the sermon, and said: Indeed the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade fasting these two days: as for the day of Fitr, it is your breaking from your fast, and as for the day of Adha, eat from the meat of your sacrifices."
"164","Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utbah, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Do not extol me as the Christians extolled Isa son of Maryam, peace upon him, for I am only a servant, so say: His servant and His Messenger."
"165","Sufyan narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, that he asked the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: Does one of us sleep while he is junub? He said: He performs ablution and sleeps if he wishes. And Sufyan once said: Let him perform ablution and sleep."
"166","Sufyan narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, that Umar gave a horse in the path of Allah, then he saw it or some of its offspring being sold, and wanted to buy it, so he asked the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about it, and he said: Leave it, it will meet you all together. And he said it twice, and he forbade him and said: Do not buy it and do not take back your charity."
"167","Sufyan narrated to us, from Asim ibn Ubayd Allah, from Abd Allah ibn Amir ibn Rabi'ah, narrating from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, reaching with it the Prophet, and Sufyan once said: From the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he said: Follow between Hajj and Umrah, for succession between them removes poverty and sins as the bellows removes the dross of iron."
"168","Sufyan narrated to us, from Yahya, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Taymi, from Alqamah ibn Waqqas, he said: I heard Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, say: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: Deeds are only by intention, and to every man is what he intended; so whoever's hijrah was to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, his hijrah is to what he migrated to, and whoever's hijrah was for a world he attains or a woman he marries, his hijrah is to what he migrated to."
"169","Sufyan narrated to us, from Abdah ibn Abi Lubabah, from Abu Wa'il, he said: al-Subayy ibn Ma'bad said: I was a Christian man, then I became Muslim, and I pronounced Hajj and Umrah together, and Zayd ibn Suhan and Salman ibn Rabi'ah heard me pronouncing them both, and they said: This one is more astray than his family's camel. So it was as though a mountain was laid upon me by their words. So I came to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and informed him, and he turned to them and blamed them, and turned to me and said: You have been guided to the sunnah of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him; you have been guided to the sunnah of your Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. Abdah said: Abu Wa'il said: Many times I and Masruq went to al-Subayy asking him about it."
"170","Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas: It was mentioned to Umar that Samurah, and once he said: It reached Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that Samurah sold wine. He said: May Allah fight Samurah; indeed the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: May Allah curse the Jews; fats were forbidden to them, so they melted them and sold them."
"171","Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr and Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Malik ibn Aws ibn al-Hadathan, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The properties of Banu al-Nadir were of what Allah granted to His Messenger, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, of what the Muslims did not spur over with horses or camels; so it belonged to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, exclusively, and he used to spend from it upon his family a year's provision, and once he said: A year's sustenance, and what remained he placed in horses and weapons as preparation in the path of Allah, Mighty and Majestic."
"172","Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from al-Zuhri, from Malik ibn Aws, he said: I heard Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, say to Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf and Talhah and al-Zubayr and Sa'd: I adjure you by Allah by Whom the heaven and the earth stand: Do you know that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: We are not inherited; what we leave is charity? They said: O Allah, yes."
"173","Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Yazid, from his father, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The child belongs to the bed."
"174","Ibn Idris narrated to us: Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ibn Abi Ammar, from Abd Allah ibn Babayh, from Ya'la ibn Umayyah, he said: I asked Umar ibn al-Khattab: There is no blame upon you to shorten prayer if you fear that those who disbelieve may afflict you, though Allah has made the people safe. So Umar said to me: I wondered at what you wondered at, so I asked the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about that, and he said: It is a charity Allah has given you, so accept His charity."
"175","Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us: al-A'mash narrated to us, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, he said: A man came to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, while he was at Arafah. He said: Abu Mu'awiyah, and al-A'mash narrated to us, from Khaythamah, from Qays ibn Marwan, that he came to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: I came, O Commander of the Believers, from Kufah, and left there a man dictating the masahif from memory. So he became angry and swelled until he almost filled what is between the two sides of the saddle, and said: And who is he, woe to you? He said: Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud. So his anger kept subsiding and being relieved until he returned to the state he was in. Then he said: Woe to you; by Allah, I know no one remaining of the people more entitled to that than he, and I will narrate to you about that: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, would continually converse at Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, such a night in a matter of the Muslims' affairs, and indeed he conversed with him one night while I was with him, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, went out, and we went out with him, and behold a man standing praying in the mosque, so the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, stood listening to his recitation, and when we were about to recognize him, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever is pleased to recite the Quran fresh as it was sent down, let him recite it upon the recitation of Ibn Umm Abd. He said: Then the man sat supplicating, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, began to say to him: Ask, you will be given; ask, you will be given. He said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I said: By Allah, I will surely go to him in the morning and congratulate him. He said: So I went to him in the morning to congratulate him, and found that Abu Bakr had preceded me to him and congratulated him; and by Allah, I never raced him to any good but he preceded me to it."
"176","Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, from Ibrahim, from Abis ibn Rabi'ah, he said: I saw Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, kiss the Stone and say: Indeed I kiss you, and I know that you are a stone, and were it not that I saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, kiss you, I would not have kissed you."
"177","Jarir narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Jabir ibn Samurah, he said: Umar addressed the people at al-Jabiyah and said: Indeed the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, stood in the like of this station of mine and said: Do good to my companions, then those who follow them, then those who follow them, then there will come a people whose one swears to an oath before he is asked to swear it, and testifies to testimony before he is asked for it. So whoever of you loves to attain the center of Paradise, let him hold to the community, for Satan is with the one, and he is farther from the two. And let no man be alone with a woman, for indeed their third is Satan. And whoever of you is pleased by his good deed and grieved by his bad deed, he is a believer."
"178","Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us: al-A'mash narrated to us, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to converse at Abu Bakr such a night in a matter of the Muslims' affairs, and I was with him."
"179","Isma'il narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah, from Qatadah, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Ma'dan ibn Abi Talhah, he said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I never asked the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about anything more than I asked him about the kalalah, until he poked with his finger in my chest and said: The summer verse at the end of Surah al-Nisa suffices you."
"180","Yahya narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated to us: Qatadah narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he said: The dead is punished in his grave by wailing over him."
"181","Yahya narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik: Abd Allah, the freedman of Asma, narrated to us, he said: Asma sent me to Ibn Umar that it reached her that you forbid three things: the figure on the garment, the purple saddle cloth, and fasting all of Rajab. So he said: As for what you mentioned of fasting Rajab, how about one who fasts perpetually? And as for what you mentioned of the figure on the garment, indeed I heard Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, say: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: Whoever wears silk in this world will not wear it in the Hereafter."
"182","Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, and I asked him: Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us: Thabit narrated to us, from Anas, he said: We were with Umar between Makkah and Madinah, and we sighted the crescent, and I was sharp-sighted and saw it, and I began to say to Umar: Do you not see it? He said: I will see it while reclining on my bed. Then he began to narrate to us about the people of Badr. He said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to show us their battle spots yesterday, saying: This is the battle spot of so-and-so tomorrow, if Allah, exalted is He, wills, and this is the battle spot of so-and-so tomorrow, if Allah, exalted is He, wills. He said: So they fell upon them. He said: I said: By Him who sent you with the truth, they did not miss those spots; they fell upon them. Then he ordered them and they were thrown into a well, and he went to them and said: O so-and-so, O so-and-so, have you found what Allah promised you true? For indeed I found what Allah promised me true. Umar said: O Messenger of Allah, do you speak to people who have rotted? He said: You do not hear what I say better than they, but they cannot answer."
"183","Yahya narrated to us: Husayn al-Mu'allim narrated to us: Amr ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: When Amr returned, the sons of Ma'mar ibn Habib came disputing with him about the wala of their sister to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, so he said: I will judge between you with what I heard from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: Whatever the child or the parent secures belongs to his asabah, whoever they are. So he judged it for us."
"184","He said: I recited to Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Uthman ibn Ghiyath: Abd Allah ibn Buraydah narrated to me, from Yahya ibn Ya'mar and Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Himyari, they said: We met Abd Allah ibn Umar and mentioned qadar and what they say about it, so he said: When you return to them, say: Indeed Ibn Umar is free of you and you are free of him, three times. Then he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, informed me that while they were sitting with the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, a man came walking, handsome of face, handsome of hair, wearing white clothes; the people looked at one another: We do not know this one, and this one is no traveler. Then he said: O Messenger of Allah, may I come to you? He said: Yes. So he came, placed his knees at his knees and his hands upon his thighs, and said: What is Islam? He said: Testimony that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, and you establish prayer, give zakah, fast Ramadan, and perform Hajj to the House. He said: Then what is iman? He said: That you believe in Allah, His angels, Paradise and the Fire, resurrection after death, and all of qadar. He said: Then what is ihsan? He said: That you act for Allah as though you see Him, for if you do not see Him, indeed He sees you. He said: Then when is the Hour? He said: The one asked about it knows no more than the asker. He said: Then what are its signs? He said: When the naked, barefoot, poor shepherds of sheep compete in buildings, and slavewomen bear their mistresses. He said: Then he said: Bring the man to me. So they sought him and saw nothing. So he remained two or three days, then he said: O Ibn al-Khattab, do you know who the asker about such and such was? He said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: That was Jibril; he came to teach you your religion. He said: And a man of Juhaynah or Muzaynah asked him and said: O Messenger of Allah, in what do we act: In something already spent or past, or in something begun anew now? He said: In something already spent or past. So a man, or some of the people, said: O Messenger of Allah, then in what do we act? He said: The people of Paradise are eased to the deeds of the people of Paradise, and the people of the Fire are eased to the deeds of the people of the Fire. Yahya said: It is thus, meaning as you recited to me."
"185","Yahya narrated to us, from Shu'bah: Salamah ibn Kuhayl narrated to me, he said: I heard Abu al-Hakam, he said: I asked Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, about the nabidh of the jar and the gourd, so he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade the nabidh of the jar and the gourd, and said: Whoever is pleased to forbid what Allah, exalted is He, and His Messenger forbade, let him forbid nabidh. He said: And I asked Ibn al-Zubayr, so he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade the gourd and the jar. He said: And I asked Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, so he narrated from Umar that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade the gourd and the muzaffat. He said: And my brother narrated to me, from Abu Sa'id, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade the jar, the gourd, the muzaffat, and unripe dates and ripe dates."
"186","Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, I asked him: Hisham narrated to us: Qatadah narrated to us, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Ma'dan ibn Abi Talhah, that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, addressed on Friday and mentioned the Prophet of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and mentioned Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: Indeed I saw as though a rooster pecked me twice, and I deem it only the coming of my term, and some people urge me to appoint a successor, yet Allah would not waste His religion nor His caliphate with which He sent His Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him; so if a matter hastens me, the caliphate is consultation among these six with whom the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, died while he was pleased with them. And indeed I knew that people will impugn this matter; I struck them with this hand of mine for Islam, and if they do, they are the enemies of Allah, the disbelieving misguided. And indeed I leave after me nothing more important to me than the kalalah, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was never harsher to me in anything since I accompanied him than he was harsh to me about the kalalah, and I never reviewed with him anything as I reviewed with him about the kalalah, until he poked with his finger in my chest and said: O Umar, does the summer verse at the end of Surah al-Nisa not suffice you? So if I live I will judge in it a judgment by which whoever recites the Quran and whoever does not recite the Quran will judge. Then he said: O Allah, indeed I call You to witness over the governors of the cities, for I only sent them to teach the people their religion and the sunnah of their Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and to divide among them their fay, and to do justice to them, and to raise to me what is difficult for them of their affair. O people, indeed you eat from two trees I deem only foul; indeed I saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, when he found their smell from a man in the mosque, order him taken by his hand and expelled to al-Baqi, and whoever eats them, let him kill them by cooking."
"187","Abd Allah ibn Numayr narrated to us, from Mujalid, from Amir, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, say to Talhah ibn Ubayd Allah: Why do I see you unkempt and dusty since the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, died? Perhaps, O Talhah, the rule of your cousin grieved you? He said: God forbid; indeed I am the least of you to do that. Indeed I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: Indeed I know a word none says at the presence of death but his soul finds relief in it when it leaves his body, and it will be a light for him on the Day of Resurrection; yet I did not ask the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about it, and he did not inform me of it, and that is what entered me. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: So I know it. He said: Then praise be to Allah; so what is it? He said: It is the word he said to his uncle: There is no god but Allah. Talhah said: You spoke truthfully."
"188","Ja'far ibn Awn narrated to us: Abu Umays informed us, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab, he said: A man of the Jews came to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: O Commander of the Believers, indeed you recite a verse in your Book; had it come upon us, the community of the Jews, we would have taken that day as a festival. He said: And which verse is it? He said: His saying, Mighty and Majestic: Today I have completed for you your religion and perfected upon you My favor. He said: So Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: By Allah, indeed I know the day in which it came down upon the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and the hour in which it came down upon the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: the evening of Arafah on Friday."
"189","Waki narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Ayyash ibn Abi Rabi'ah, may Allah be pleased with them, from Hakim ibn Hakim ibn Abbad ibn Hunayf, from Abu Umamah ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf, that a man shot a man with an arrow and killed him, and he had no heir except a maternal uncle, so Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah wrote about that to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and he wrote: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Allah and His Messenger are the patron of whoever has no patron, and the maternal uncle is the heir of whoever has no heir."
"190","Waki narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Ya'fur al-Abdi, he said: I heard an elder in Makkah during the rule of al-Hajjaj narrate from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to him: O Umar, indeed you are a strong man; do not crowd at the Stone lest you harm the weak; if you find vacancy, touch it, otherwise face it, and say tahlil and takbir."
"191","Waki narrated to us: Kahmas narrated to us, from Ibn Buraydah, from Yahya ibn Ya'mar, from Ibn Umar, that Jibril, peace upon him, said to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: What is iman? He said: That you believe in Allah, His angels, His Books, His Messengers, the Last Day, and qadar, its good and its evil. So Jibril, peace upon him, said to him: You spoke truthfully. He said: So we marveled at him: He asks him and confirms him. He said: So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: That was Jibril; he came to teach you the landmarks of your religion."
"192","Waki narrated to us: Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to us, from his father Urwah, from Asim ibn Umar, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: When the night comes, and once he said: The night comes from here and the day goes from here, the fasting person has broken fast, meaning the east and the west."
"193","Yazid narrated to us: Isra'il ibn Yunus informed us, from Abd al-A'la al-Tha'labi, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, he said: I was with Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and a man came to him and said: Indeed I saw the crescent, the crescent of Shawwal. So Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: O people, break fast. Then he stood to a vessel in which was water, performed ablution, and wiped over his two leather socks. So the man said: By Allah, O Commander of the Believers, I came to you only to ask you about this; did you see another do it? So he said: Yes, one better than me and the best of the ummah; I saw Abu al-Qasim, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, do the like of what I did, wearing a Syrian cloak narrow in the sleeves, so he inserted his hand from under the cloak. Then Umar prayed Maghrib."
"194","Muhammad ibn Ja'far narrated to us: Sa'id narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Sulayman, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Indeed the Prophet of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, did not forbid the lizard, but he found it distasteful. And other than Muhammad said: From Sulayman al-Yashkuri."
"195","Muhammad ibn Ja'far narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Asim ibn Ubayd Allah, from Salim, from Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, that he sought his permission for Umrah, and he permitted him, and said: O brother, do not forget us in your supplication. And he said afterward in Madinah: O brother, include us in your supplication. So Umar said: I would not love to have for it all upon which the sun rises, for his saying: O brother."
"196","Muhammad ibn Ja'far narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated to us, and Hajjaj said: I heard Shu'bah, from Asim ibn Ubayd Allah, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he said to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: What do you think of what we act in: Is it already finished, or something begun anew? He said: In what is already finished. So Umar said: Shall we then rely? So he said: Act, O Ibn al-Khattab, for each is eased: As for whoever was of the people of happiness, he will act for happiness, and as for the people of wretchedness, he will act for wretchedness."
"197","Hushaym narrated to us: al-Zuhri narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Utbah ibn Mas'ud: Abd Allah ibn Abbas informed me: Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf narrated to me that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, addressed the people and he heard him say: Indeed people say: What is the matter with stoning in the Book of Allah, flogging? Yet the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, stoned, and we stoned after him, and were it not that men would say, or a speaker would speak, that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, added to the Book of Allah what is not of it, I would have affirmed it as it was sent down."
"198","Muhammad ibn Ja'far narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Khumayr narrate from Habib ibn Ubayd, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Ibn al-Simt, that he came to a land called Dawmin of Hims, eighteen miles away, and prayed two rak'ahs. So I said to him: Do you pray two rak'ahs? So he said: I saw Umar ibn al-Khattab at Dhu al-Hulayfah pray two rak'ahs, and I asked him, so he said: I only do as I saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, do, or he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, did."
"199","Abu Abd al-Rahman said: Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us: I recited to Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi: Malik, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim ibn Abd Allah, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: A man of the companions of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, entered the mosque on Friday while Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, was addressing the people, so Umar said: What hour is this? So he said: O Commander of the Believers, I returned from the market and heard the call, and did no more than perform ablution. So Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: And ablution also, though you knew that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to command ghusl."
"200","Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Maymun, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The polytheists would not depart from Muzdalifah until the sun rose over Thabir, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, opposed them and departed before the sun rose."
"201","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj informed us, Abu al-Zubayr narrated to me that he heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah saying: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, informed me that he heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: I will surely expel the Jews and the Christians from the Arabian Peninsula until I leave none but a Muslim."
"202","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Mamar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, while he was standing delivering the sermon on Friday, a man from among the Companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, entered, so Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, called out to him: What time is this? He said: I was busy today and did not return to my family until I heard the call to prayer, and I did no more than perform ablution. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Ablution as well, when you know - and in another place: when you know - that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to command full washing."
"203","Hashim ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, Ikrimah, meaning Ibn Ammar, narrated to us, Simak al-Hanafi Abu Zumayl narrated to me, saying: Abdullah ibn Abbas narrated to me, Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me, saying: On the day of Khaybar, a group of the Companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came forward saying: So-and-so is a martyr, so-and-so is a martyr, until they passed by a man and said: So-and-so is a martyr. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: No indeed, I have seen him in the Fire for a cloak he misappropriated, or an outer garment. Then the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: O son of al-Khattab, go and call out among the people that none shall enter Paradise except the believers. He said: So I went out and called out: Indeed none shall enter Paradise except the believers."
"204","Abdullah ibn Yazid narrated to us, Dawud, meaning Ibn Abi al-Furat, narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Buraydah narrated to me, from Abu al-Aswad al-Dili, saying: I came to Medina when a sickness had fallen upon it and they were dying a swift death, and I sat beside Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and a funeral procession passed by him and its owner was praised with good, so Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: It has become due. Then another passed and its owner was praised with good, so he said: It has become due. Then a third passed and its owner was praised with evil, so Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: It has become due. I said: What is it that has become due, O Commander of the Believers? He said: I said as the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Any Muslim for whom four bear witness to good, Allah admits him to Paradise. He said: We said: Or three? He said: Or three. We said: Or two? He said: Or two. Then we did not ask him about one."
"205","Abu Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Haywah narrated to us, Bakr ibn Amr informed me that he heard Abdullah ibn Hubayrah saying that he heard Abu Tamim al-Jayshani saying: He heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, saying that he heard the Prophet of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: If you were to rely upon Allah with His true reliance, He would provide for you as He provides for the birds; they go out in the morning hungry and return full."
"206","Abu Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Said ibn Abi Ayyub narrated to me, Ata ibn Dinar narrated to me, from Hakim ibn Sharik al-Hudhali, from Yahya ibn Maymun al-Hadrami, from Rabiah al-Jurashi, from Abu Hurayrah, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: Do not sit with the people of predestinarian denial, and do not engage them in discourse. And Abu Abd al-Rahman once said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"207","Hashim ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Khumayr al-Hamdani Abu Umar, saying: I heard Habib ibn Ubayd narrating from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Ibn al-Simt, that he went out with Umar to Dhu al-Hulayfah and he prayed two units, so I asked him about that, and he said: I only do as I saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, do."
"208","Abu Nuh Qurad narrated to us, Ikrimah ibn Ammar informed us, Simak al-Hanafi Abu Zumayl narrated to us, Ibn Abbas narrated to me, Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me, saying: On the day of Badr he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, looked at his Companions, and they were three hundred and some, and looked at the polytheists, and they were a thousand and more, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, faced the qiblah, then stretched out his hands, with his cloak and waist-wrap upon him, then said: O Allah, where is what You promised me, O Allah, fulfill what You promised me, O Allah, if You destroy this band of the people of Islam, You will never be worshipped on earth again. He said: He kept beseeching his Lord, mighty and majestic, and calling upon Him, until his cloak fell, so Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, came to him, took his cloak and put it back on him, then embraced him from behind, then said: O Prophet of Allah, your earnest pleading to your Lord suffices you, for He will fulfill for you what He promised you. And Allah, mighty and majestic, revealed: When you sought aid from your Lord and He answered you that I am reinforcing you with a thousand of the angels following one another. On that day when they met, Allah, mighty and majestic, routed the polytheists, and seventy men of them were killed and seventy men of them were taken captive. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, consulted Abu Bakr and Ali and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: O Prophet of Allah, these are sons of uncles and kin and brothers, and I see that you take ransom from them, so what we take from them will be strength for us against the disbelievers, and perhaps Allah will guide them so they become support for us. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: What do you see, O son of al-Khattab? He said: I said: By Allah, I do not see what Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, saw, but I see that you give me power over so-and-so, a relative of Umar, so I strike his neck, and give Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, power over Aqil so he strikes his neck, and give Hamzah power over so-and-so his brother so he strikes his neck, so that Allah knows that there is no tenderness in our hearts for the polytheists; these are their chiefs and leaders and commanders. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, inclined to what Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said, and did not incline to what I said, so he took ransom from them. The next day Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I went early to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and there he was sitting with Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and they were weeping. I said: O Messenger of Allah, tell me what makes you and your companion weep; if I find weeping I will weep, and if I do not find weeping I will force weeping for your weeping. He said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: What your companions proposed to me of ransom - your punishment was presented to me nearer than this tree, for a nearby tree. And Allah, mighty and majestic, revealed: It is not for a prophet to have captives until he has thoroughly subdued in the land, up to His saying: Had it not been for a prior decree from Allah, there would have touched you for what you took of ransom. Then spoils were made lawful for them. On the day of Uhud in the following year they were punished for what they did on the day of Badr in taking ransom: seventy of them were killed, and the Companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, fled from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and his front tooth was broken, and the helmet was smashed upon his head, and blood flowed upon his face, and Allah the Exalted revealed: When a calamity befell you though you had inflicted twice as much - the verse - for your taking ransom."
"209","Abu Nuh narrated to us, Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: We were with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, on a journey. He said: I asked him about something three times and he did not answer me. He said: I said to myself: May your mother be bereft of you, O son of al-Khattab, you pressed the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, three times and he did not answer you. He said: So I mounted my riding camel and went ahead, fearing something had been revealed concerning me. He said: And there was a caller calling: O Umar, where is Umar? He said: So I returned, thinking something had been revealed concerning me. He said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: There was revealed to me last night a chapter dearer to me than the world and what is in it: Indeed We have granted you a clear victory, that Allah may forgive you what preceded of your sin and what followed."
"210","Abu al-Nadr narrated to us, al-Masudi narrated to us, from Hakim ibn Jubayr, from Musa ibn Talhah, from Ibn al-Hawtakiyyah, saying: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, was brought food and invited a man to it, and he said: I am fasting. Then he said: What fasting do you fast? Were it not for dislike of adding or omitting, I would have narrated to you the account of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, when the bedouin came to him with the hare. But send for Ammar. When Ammar came he said: Did you witness the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, on the day the bedouin came to him with the hare? He said: Yes. He said: I saw blood in it. He said: Eat it. He said: I am fasting. He said: What fasting do you fast? He said: The beginning of the month and its end. He said: If you are fasting, then fast the thirteenth and the fourteenth and the fifteenth."
"211","Abu al-Nadr narrated to us, Abu Aqil narrated to us, Mujalid ibn Said narrated to us, Amir informed us, from Masruq ibn al-Ajda, saying: I met Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and he said to me: Who are you? I said: Masruq ibn al-Ajda. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: Al-Ajda is a devil, but you are Masruq ibn Abd al-Rahman. Amir said: So I saw him in the register written as Masruq ibn Abd al-Rahman, and I said: What is this? He said: Thus Umar named me."
"212","Ishaq ibn Isa narrated to us, Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Jafar ibn Rabiah, from al-Zuhri, from Muharrar ibn Abi Hurayrah, from his father, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade withdrawal from the free woman except with her permission."
"213","Abu Amir Abd al-Malik ibn Amr narrated to us, saying: Hisham, meaning Ibn Sad, narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, saying: I heard Umar saying: If I live to this coming year, no town shall be opened for the people but I shall divide it among them, as the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, divided Khaybar."
"214","Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Zubayri narrated to us, Israil narrated to us, from Simak, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: I was with the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, on a campaign, and I swore: No, by my father. A man behind me called out to me and said: Do not swear by your fathers, and behold it was the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"215","Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: If I live, Allah willing, I will surely expel the Jews and the Christians from the Arabian Peninsula."
"216","Sulayman ibn Dawud Abu Dawud narrated to us, Sharik narrated to us, from Asim ibn Ubayd Allah, from his father, from Umar, saying: I saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, wiping over the two leather socks."
"217","Sulayman ibn Dawud Abu Dawud narrated to us, Sallam, meaning Abu al-Ahwas, narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Sayyar ibn al-Marur, saying: I heard Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, delivering the sermon, saying: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, built this mosque, with us alongside him, the Emigrants and the Helpers. When crowding becomes severe, let a man of you prostrate upon the back of his brother. And he saw people praying in the road, so he said: Pray in the mosque."
"218","He said: I read to Yahya ibn Said, from Zuhayr, saying: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Harithah ibn Mudrib, that he performed pilgrimage with Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and the nobles of the people of Syria came to him and said: O Commander of the Believers, we have acquired from our wealth slaves and mounts, so take from our wealth charity by which you purify us, to be alms for us. He said: This is something the two who were before me did not do, but wait until I consult the Muslims."
"219","Rawh and Muammal narrated to us, saying: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: If I live, I will surely expel the Jews and the Christians from the Arabian Peninsula until I leave in it none but a Muslim."
"220","Attab ibn Ziyad narrated to us, Abdullah, meaning Ibn al-Mubarak, narrated to us, Yunus informed us, from al-Zuhri, from al-Saib ibn Yazid and Ubayd Allah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him - Abdullah said: And my father traced it to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - saying: Whoever misses something of his nightly portion - or he said: of his portion - of the night, then recites it between the dawn prayer and the noon prayer, it is as if he recited it in his night."
"221","Abu Nuh Qurad narrated to us, Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, Simak al-Hanafi Abu Zumayl narrated to us, Ibn Abbas narrated to me, Umar narrated to me, saying: On the day of Badr he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, looked at his Companions, and they were three hundred and some, and looked at the polytheists, and they were a thousand and more, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, faced the qiblah, then stretched out his hand, with his cloak and waist-wrap upon him, then said: O Allah, where is what You promised me, O Allah, fulfill what You promised me, O Allah, if You destroy this band of the people of Islam, You will never be worshipped on earth again. He said: He kept beseeching his Lord and calling upon Him until his cloak fell, so Abu Bakr came to him, took his cloak and put it back on him, then embraced him from behind, then said: O Prophet of Allah, your earnest pleading to your Lord suffices you, for He will fulfill for you what He promised you. And Allah the Exalted revealed: When you sought aid from your Lord and He answered you that I am reinforcing you with a thousand of the angels following one another. On that day when they met, Allah routed the polytheists, and seventy men of them were killed and seventy men of them were taken captive. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, consulted Abu Bakr and Ali and Umar. Abu Bakr said: O Prophet of Allah, these are sons of uncles and kin and brothers, and I see that you take ransom from them, so what we take from them will be strength for us against the disbelievers, and perhaps Allah, mighty and majestic, will guide them so they become support for us. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: What do you see, O son of al-Khattab? He said: I said: By Allah, I do not see what Abu Bakr saw, but I see that you give me power over so-and-so, a relative of Umar, so I strike his neck, and give Ali power over Aqil so he strikes his neck, and give Hamzah power over so-and-so his brother so he strikes his neck, so that Allah knows that there is no tenderness in our hearts for the polytheists; these are their chiefs and leaders and commanders. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, inclined to what Abu Bakr said, and did not incline to what I said, so he took ransom from them. The next day Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I went early to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and there he was sitting with Abu Bakr, and they were weeping. I said: O Messenger of Allah, tell me what makes you and your companion weep; if I find weeping I will weep, and if I do not find weeping I will force weeping for your weeping. He said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: What your companions proposed to me of ransom - indeed your punishment was presented to me nearer than this tree, for a nearby tree. And Allah the Exalted revealed: It is not for a prophet to have captives until he has thoroughly subdued in the land, up to His saying: There would have touched you for what you took of ransom. Then spoils were made lawful for them. On the day of Uhud in the following year they were punished for what they did on the day of Badr in taking ransom: seventy of them were killed, and the Companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, fled from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and his front tooth was broken, and the helmet was smashed upon his head, and blood flowed upon his face, so Allah revealed: When a calamity befell you though you had inflicted twice as much, up to His saying: Indeed Allah is over all things Competent, for your taking ransom."
"222","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Thawr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, saying: I remained keen to ask Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, about the two women among the wives of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about whom Allah the Exalted said: If you both repent to Allah, for your hearts have inclined, until Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, performed pilgrimage and I performed pilgrimage with him. When we were on part of the road, Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, turned aside, and I turned aside with him with the water vessel. He relieved himself, then came to me, and I poured water upon his hands and he performed ablution. I said: O Commander of the Believers, who are the two women among the wives of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about whom Allah the Exalted said: If you both repent to Allah, for your hearts have inclined? Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: How strange of you, O son of Abbas. Al-Zuhri said: By Allah, he disliked what he asked him about, yet did not conceal it from him. He said: She is Hafsah and Aishah. He said: Then he began relating the account. He said: We, the company of Quraysh, were a people who dominated women. When we came to Medina we found a people dominated by their women, and our women began learning from their women. He said: My dwelling was among Banu Umayyah ibn Zayd at al-Awali. He said: One day I became angry with my wife, and she answered me back. I disapproved that she answer me back, so she said: Why do you disapprove that I answer you back? By Allah, the wives of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, answer him back, and one of them forsakes him from day to night. He said: So I set out and entered upon Hafsah and said: Do you answer back the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him? She said: Yes. I said: And does one of you forsake him from day to night? She said: Yes. I said: Whoever of you does that has failed and lost. Does one of you feel safe that Allah become angry with her for the anger of His Messenger, so she is ruined? Do not answer back the Messenger of Allah, and do not ask him anything, and ask me whatever occurs to you, and let not your neighbor deceive you, if she is more beautiful and more beloved to the Messenger of Allah than you - meaning Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her. He said: I had a neighbor from the Helpers, and we used to take turns going down to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: he would go down one day and I would go down one day, so he would bring me news of revelation and other matters, and I would bring him the like. He said: We used to say that Ghassan were shoeing horses to raid us. My companion went down one day, then came to me in the evening, knocked on my door, then called me. I went out to him and he said: A grave matter has happened. I said: What is it, has Ghassan come? He said: No, rather greater than that and longer: the Messenger has divorced his wives. I said: Hafsah has failed and lost; I had thought this would happen. When I prayed the dawn prayer I tightened my clothes upon me, then went down and entered upon Hafsah while she was weeping. I said: Has the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, divorced you? She said: I do not know; there he is, secluded in this upper room. I came to a black boy of his and said: Seek permission for Umar. The boy entered, then came out to me and said: I mentioned you to him and he was silent. I set out until I came to the pulpit, and there was a group sitting beside it, some of them weeping. I sat a little, then what I felt overcame me, so I came to the boy and said: Seek permission for Umar. The boy entered, then came out to me and said: I mentioned you to him and he was silent. I went out and sat beside the pulpit, then what I felt overcame me, so I came to the boy and said: Seek permission for Umar. He entered, then came out to me and said: I mentioned you to him and he was silent. I turned away going, and the boy called me, saying: Enter, for he has permitted you. I entered and greeted the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and there he was reclining upon the woven fibers of a mat - and Yaqub narrated it to us in the account of Salih, saying: the fibers of a mat - which had left marks on his side. I said: Have you divorced your wives, O Messenger of Allah? He raised his head to me and said: No. I said: Allah is Greatest. If only you had seen us, O Messenger of Allah: we, the company of Quraysh, were a people who dominated women. When we came to Medina we found a people dominated by their women, and our women began learning from their women. One day I became angry with my wife, and she answered me back. I disapproved that she answer me back, so she said: Why do you disapprove that I answer you back? By Allah, the wives of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, answer him back, and one of them forsakes him from day to night. I said: Whoever of them does that has failed and lost. Does one of them feel safe that Allah become angry with her for the anger of His Messenger, so she is ruined? The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, smiled. I said: O Messenger of Allah, I entered upon Hafsah and said: Let not your neighbor deceive you, if she is more beautiful and more beloved to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, than you. He smiled again. I said: May I feel at ease, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Yes. I sat, then raised my head in the house, and by Allah I saw in it nothing to catch the eye except three hides. I said: Supplicate, O Messenger of Allah, that He widen provision for your community, for provision has been widened for Persia and Rome though they do not worship Allah. He sat upright, then said: Are you in doubt, O son of al-Khattab? Those are a people whose good things were hastened for them in the life of this world. I said: Seek forgiveness for me, O Messenger of Allah. And he had sworn not to enter upon them for a month, from the severity of his displeasure with them, until Allah, mighty and majestic, reproached him."
"223","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Yunus ibn Sulaym informed me, saying: Yunus ibn Yazid al-Ayli dictated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: When revelation descended upon the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, a humming like the humming of bees would be heard at his face. We remained a while, then he faced the qiblah and raised his hands and said: O Allah, increase us and do not decrease us, honor us and do not humiliate us, give us and do not deprive us, favor us and do not favor others over us, be pleased with us and make us pleased. Then he said: There were revealed to me ten verses; whoever upholds them enters Paradise. Then he recited to us: Successful indeed are the believers, until he completed the ten verses."
"224","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Ubayd, freedman of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, that he witnessed the festival with Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and he prayed before delivering the sermon, without call to prayer or commencement, then delivered the sermon and said: O people, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade fasting these two days: as for one of them, it is the day of your breaking fast from your fasting, and your festival; as for the other, it is a day in which you eat from your sacrifices."
"225","Yaqub narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, al-Zuhri narrated to us, from Sad ibn Abi Ubayd, freedman of Abd al-Rahman ibn Azhar, saying: I witnessed the festival with Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and he mentioned the account."
"226","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Umar narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, kissed the Stone, then said: I know you are a stone, and had I not seen the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, kiss you, I would not have kissed you."
"227","Hushaym narrated to us, Sayyar informed me, from Abu Wail, that a man who was a Christian called al-Subayy ibn Mabad embraced Islam and intended striving in the way of Allah, so it was said to him: Begin with pilgrimage. He came to al-Ashari and he commanded him to enter ritual consecration for pilgrimage and lesser pilgrimage together, so he did. While he was chanting the response, Yazid ibn Sawhan and Salman ibn Rabiah passed, and one of them said to his companion: This one is more astray than his people's camel. Al-Subayy heard it and it weighed heavily upon him. When he arrived he came to Umar and mentioned that to him, so Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: You were guided to the practice of your Prophet. He said: And I heard him another time saying: You were granted success to the practice of your Prophet."
"228","Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, al-Amash narrated to us, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, would converse at night with Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, likewise, concerning some affair of the Muslims, with me alongside him."
"229","Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, Asim al-Ahwal narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Sarjis, saying: I saw al-Usayli, meaning Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, kissing the Stone and saying: I kiss you, knowing you are a stone that neither benefits nor harms, and had I not seen the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, kiss you, I would not have kissed you."
"230","Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, Ubayd Allah narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him: I said: O Messenger of Allah, does one of us sleep while ritually impure? He said: Yes, when he performs ablution."
"231","Ibn Numayr narrated to us, Hisham informed us, from his father, from Asim, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: When night comes and day departs and the sun sets, you have broken fast."
"232","Abu Kamil narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, Ibn Shihab narrated to us - and Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri - the meaning, from Abu al-Tufayl Amir ibn Wathilah, that Nafi ibn Abd al-Harith met Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, at Usfan - and Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, had employed him over Mecca - so Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: Whom have you left in charge of the people of the valley? He said: I left Ibn Abza in charge of them. He said: What is Ibn Abza? He said: A man from among our freedmen. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: You left a freedman in charge of them? He said: He is a reciter of the Book of Allah, learned in the obligations, a judge. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Indeed your Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Indeed Allah raises peoples by this Book and lowers others by it."
"233","Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, Ismail ibn Sumay narrated to us, from Muslim al-Batin, from Abu al-Bakhtari, saying: Umar said to Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah: Stretch out your hand so I may pledge allegiance to you, for I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: You are the trustee of this community. Abu Ubaydah said: I would not step forward before a man whom the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, commanded to lead us, and he led us until he died."
"234","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Sufyan informed us, from al-Amash, from Shaqiq ibn Salamah, from Salman ibn Rabiah, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, divided a distribution, so I said: O Messenger of Allah, others than these are more deserving of it than them. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: They gave me the choice between asking me insolently or deeming me miserly, and I am not miserly."
"235","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Umar informed us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, asked the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: Does one of us sleep while ritually impure? He said: Yes, and he performs his ablution for prayer."
"236","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, asked the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, the like of it."
"237","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Umar informed us, from Nafi, saying: Ibn Umar saw Sad ibn Malik wiping over his two leather socks, so Ibn Umar said: Do you indeed do this? Sad said: Yes. They gathered with Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and Sad said: O Commander of the Believers, give a ruling to my nephew concerning wiping over the two leather socks. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: When we were with our Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, we used to wipe over our leather socks. Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Even if one comes from relieving himself and urinating? Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Yes, even if one comes from relieving himself and urinating. Nafi said: After that Ibn Umar used to wipe over them so long as he did not take them off, and did not set a time limit for that. I narrated it to Mamar, and he said: Ayyub narrated it to me, from Nafi, the like of it."
"238","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri: Malik ibn Aws ibn al-Hadathan informed me, saying: I exchanged silver for gold with Talhah ibn Ubayd Allah, and he said: Wait until our treasurer comes to us from al-Ghabah. He said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, heard it and said: No, by Allah, do not leave him until you take full exchange from him, for I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: Gold for silver is usury except hand to hand."
"239","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Mamar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah, saying: When the people of apostasy apostatized in the time of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, Umar said: How do you fight the people, O Abu Bakr, when the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: I was commanded to fight the people until they say: There is no god but Allah; when they say: There is no god but Allah, they protect from me their blood and their wealth except by its right, and their reckoning is upon Allah? Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: By Allah, I will surely fight whoever separates prayer from alms, for alms is the right of wealth. By Allah, had they withheld from me a young goat they used to render to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, I would have fought them for it. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: By Allah, it was only that I saw Allah had opened the breast of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, to fighting, so I knew it was the truth."
"240","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Israil informed us, from Simak, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, saying: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I was in a company traveling on a campaign with the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and I swore, saying: No, by my father. A man behind me rebuked me and said: Do not swear by your fathers. I turned, and behold it was the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"241","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Mamar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, heard me swearing by my father, so he said: Allah forbids you to swear by your fathers. Umar said: By Allah, I never swore by it afterward, neither deliberately nor quoting another."
"242","Khalaf ibn al-Walid narrated to us, Khalid narrated to us, from Khalid, from Abu Uthman, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, permitted silk to the extent of two fingers."
"243","Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, al-Taymi narrated to us, from Abu Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: We were with Utbah ibn Farqad, and Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to him of things relating from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. Among what he wrote to him was that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: None wears silk in this world except one who has no share of it in the Hereafter, except thus. And he gestured with his two fingers, the forefinger and the middle. Abu Uthman said: I thought they were the buttons of the outer cloaks when we saw the outer cloaks."
"244","Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Ammar narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Babayh, from Yala ibn Umayyah, saying: I said to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him: The shortening of prayer by people today, when Allah, mighty and majestic, only said: If you fear that those who disbelieve may persecute you - and that day has gone. He said: I wondered at what you wondered at, so I mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he said: It is a charity Allah has bestowed upon you, so accept His charity."
"245","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj informed us: I heard Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Ammar narrating, and he mentioned it."
"246","Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Arubah: Qatadah narrated to us, from Said ibn al-Musayyab, saying: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The last of the Quran to be revealed is the verse of usury, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was taken without interpreting it, so leave usury and doubt."
"247","Yahya narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, Qatadah narrated to us, from Said ibn al-Musayyab, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: The dead is punished in his grave for lamentation over him."
"248","Yahya narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah: Nafi informed me, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: The dead is punished for the weeping of his family over him."
"249","Yahya narrated to us, from Yahya, saying: I heard Said ibn al-Musayyab that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Beware lest you perish by abandoning the verse of stoning. We do not find two hadd punishments in the Book of Allah, yet I saw the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, stone, and we stoned."
"250","Yahya narrated to us, Humayd narrated to us, from Anas, saying: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I agreed with my Lord in three, and my Lord agreed with me in three. I said: O Messenger of Allah, if only you took from the standing place of Ibrahim a place of prayer. So Allah revealed: And take from the standing place of Ibrahim a place of prayer. I said: O Messenger of Allah, the righteous and the wicked enter upon you; if only you commanded the Mothers of the Believers to veil. So Allah revealed the verse of veiling. And it reached me that the Prophet, upon him peace, reproached some of his wives. He said: I visited the Mothers of the Believers one by one, questioning them. I entered upon them, questioning them one by one: By Allah, if you do not desist, Allah will surely replace His Messenger with better than you. He said: I came to one of his wives, and she said: O Umar, is there not in the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, what admonishes his wives, that you admonish them? So Allah, mighty and majestic, revealed: Perhaps his Lord, if he divorces you, will replace for him wives better than you."
"251","Yahya narrated to us, from Shubah: Abu Dhibyan narrated to me: I heard Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr saying: Do not clothe your women in silk, for I heard Umar narrating, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, that he said: Whoever wears silk in this world shall not wear it in the Hereafter. And Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr said on his own: Whoever does not wear it in the Hereafter shall not enter Paradise. Allah the Exalted said: And their clothing therein is silk."
"252","Yahya narrated to us, from Ismail: Amir narrated to us - and Muhammad ibn Ubayd narrated to us, Ismail ibn Abi Khalid narrated to us, from a man, from al-Shabi, saying: Umar passed by Talhah - and he mentioned its meaning. He said: Umar passed by Talhah and saw him grieved. He said: Perhaps the leadership of your cousin displeases you - meaning Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him. He said: No, but I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: I know a word no man says at his death but it is light in his record, or he finds relief by it at death. Umar said: I shall tell you of it: it is the word he intended for his uncle: testimony that there is no god but Allah. He said: It was as if a covering was lifted from me. He said: You spoke truly; had he known a word better than it, he would have commanded him by it."
"253","Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj: Sulayman ibn Atiq narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Babayh, from Yala ibn Umayyah, saying: I circumambulated with Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him. When I was at the corner beside the door toward the Stone, I took his hand that he might touch it. He said: Did you not circumambulate with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him? I said: Yes indeed. He said: Did you see him touch it? I said: No. He said: Then pass on from it, for you have in the Messenger of Allah a good example."
"254","Yahya narrated to us, from al-Amash: Shaqiq narrated to us: al-Subayy ibn Mabad narrated to me - and he was a man from Banu Taghlib - saying: I was a Christian, then embraced Islam, and strove diligently without falling short. I entered consecration for a pilgrimage and a lesser pilgrimage. I passed at al-Udhayb by Salman ibn Rabiah and Zayd ibn Sawhan, and one of them said: With both together? His companion said to him: Leave him, for he is more astray than his camel. It was as if my camel were upon my neck. I came to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and mentioned that to him, so Umar said to me: They said nothing amiss; you were guided to the practice of your Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"255","Yahya narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah: Nafi narrated to me, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: O Messenger of Allah, I vowed in the pre-Islamic age to seclude myself in the Sacred Mosque for a night. He said to him: Fulfill your vow."
"256","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Sufyan informed us, from Mansur, from Abu Wail, from Subayy ibn Mabad al-Taghlibi, saying: I was newly quit of Christianity, and I intended striving in the way of Allah or pilgrimage. I came to a man of my people called Hudaym and asked him, and he commanded me with pilgrimage. I joined pilgrimage and lesser pilgrimage together, and he mentioned it."
"257","Waki narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us - and Abd al-Rahman, from Sufyan, from Zubayd al-Iyami, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: The prayer of travel is two units, the prayer of sacrifice is two units, the prayer of breaking fast is two units, and the Friday prayer is two units, complete without shortening, upon the tongue of Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. Sufyan said: Zubayd once said - I think - from Umar. Abd al-Rahman said: Without any doubt. And Yazid, meaning Ibn Harun, said: Ibn Abi Layla said: I heard Umar, may Allah be pleased with him."
"258","Waki narrated to us, Hisham ibn Sad narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that he found a horse he had given to be ridden in the way of Allah being sold in the market, and he wanted to buy it, so he asked the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he forbade him, saying: Do not take back your charity."
"259","Waki narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays, saying: I saw Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, with a palm stalk in his hand, seating the people, saying: Listen to the words of the successor of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. A freedman of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, called Shadid came with a sheet and read it to the people, saying: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, says: Hear and obey what is in this sheet, for by Allah I have not fallen short for you. Qays said: Then I saw Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, upon the pulpit afterward."
"260","Muammal narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Salamah, from Imran al-Sulami, saying: I asked Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, about nabidh, and he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade nabidh of the earthen jar and the gourd. I met Ibn Umar and asked him, and he informed me - as I think - from Umar, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade nabidh of the earthen jar and the gourd. Sufyan doubted. He said: I met Ibn al-Zubayr and asked him, and he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade nabidh of the earthen jar and the gourd."
"261","Aswad ibn Amir narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Abu Sinan, from Ubayd ibn Adam and Abu Maryam and Abu Shuayb, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, was at al-Jabiyah, and he mentioned the conquest of the House of the Holy. He said: Abu Salamah said: Abu Sinan narrated to me, from Ubayd ibn Adam, saying: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, saying to Kab: Where do you think I should pray? He said: If you take from me, you will pray behind the Rock, so all of Jerusalem will be before you. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: You have resembled Judaism. No, but I pray where the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, prayed. He advanced toward the qiblah and prayed, then came and spread his cloak and swept the refuse into his cloak, and the people swept."
"262","Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Malik, meaning Ibn Mighwal, narrated to us, saying: I heard al-Fudayl ibn Amr, from Ibrahim al-Nakhai, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: I asked the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about kalalah, and he said: The summer verse suffices you. He said: That I had asked the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about it is dearer to me than possessing red camels."
"263","Abu Ahmad Muhammad ibn Abdullah narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that he came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: Ritual impurity befalls me. He commanded him to wash his private part and perform his ablution for prayer."
"264","Affan narrated to us, Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Qazaah, saying: I said to Ibn Umar: Does Allah punish this dead one for the weeping of this living one? He said: Umar narrated to me from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - I did not lie about Umar, nor did Umar lie about the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"265","Affan narrated to us, Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Ubayd Allah narrated to us, Ibrahim narrated to us, from Alqamah, from al-Qartha, from Qays - or Ibn Qays - a man from Jufi, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, saying: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, passed with me and Abu Bakr alongside him by Abdullah ibn Masud while he was reciting. He stood and heard his recitation, then Abdullah bowed and prostrated. He said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Ask, you shall be given; ask, you shall be given. He said: Then the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, went on and said: Whoever delights that he recite the Quran fresh as it was revealed, let him recite it from Ibn Umm Abd. He said: So I went early to Abdullah ibn Masud to gladden him with what the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said. He said: When I knocked on the door - or he said: when he heard my voice - he said: What brings you at this hour? I said: I came to gladden you with what the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said. He said: Abu Bakr has already preceded you. I said: If he does, he is ever foremost in good works; we never vied in good but Abu Bakr preceded us to it."
"266","Affan narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Said al-Jurayri, from Abu Nadrah, from Usayr ibn Jabir, saying: When the people of Yemen came forward, Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, began questioning the companies, saying: Is there among you anyone from Qaran, until he came upon Qaran. He said: Who are you? They said: Qaran. The bridle of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him - or the bridle of Uways - fell, and one of them handed it to the other, and he recognized him. Umar said: What is your name? He said: I am Uways. He said: Have you a mother? He said: Yes. He said: Was there any whiteness upon you? He said: Yes, then I supplicated Allah, mighty and majestic, and He removed it from me except the place of a dirham at my navel, that I might remember my Lord by it. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: Seek forgiveness for me. He said: You are more entitled to seek forgiveness for me; you are a Companion of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: The best of the successors is a man called Uways. He has a mother, and there was whiteness upon him, so he supplicated Allah, mighty and majestic, and He removed it from him except the place of a dirham at his navel. So he sought forgiveness for him, then entered among the multitude of people and it was not known where he went. He said: He came to Kufa. He said: We used to gather in a circle and remember Allah, and he would sit with us. When he spoke, his speech fell upon our hearts in a way no other's speech fell. And he mentioned the account."
"267","Abd al-Malik ibn Abi al-Shawarib narrated to us, Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Ubayd Allah narrated to us, from Ibrahim, from al-Qartha, from Qays - or Ibn Qays - a man from Jufi, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and he mentioned the like of the account of Affan."
"268","Affan narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Thabit narrated to us, from Anas, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, when Hafsah wailed over him, said: O Hafsah, did you not hear the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: The one wailed over is punished. He said: And Suhayb wailed, so Umar said: O Suhayb, do you not know that the one wailed over is punished."
"269","Affan narrated to us, Abd al-Wahid narrated to us, Yazid al-Rishk narrated to us, from Muadhah, from Umm Amr daughter of Abdullah, that she heard Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr narrating that he heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, delivering the sermon, saying: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever wears silk in this world shall not be clothed with it in the Hereafter."
"270","Affan narrated to us, Hammam narrated to us, Qatadah narrated to us, Abu al-Aliyah narrated to us, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him: Trustworthy men narrated to me, among them Umar, may Allah be pleased with him - and Affan once said: Trustworthy men testified before me, and the most pleasing of them to me is Umar, may Allah be pleased with him - that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: There is no prayer after two prayers: after dawn until the sun rises, and after the afternoon until the sun sets."
"271","Affan narrated to us, Aban narrated to us, Qatadah narrated to us, from Abu al-Aliyah, from Ibn Abbas, the like of this: Trustworthy men testified before me."
"272","Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab, that the Jews said to Umar: You recite a verse which, had it been revealed among us, we would have taken that day as a festival. He said: I know where it was revealed, and on what day it was revealed, and where the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was when it was revealed. It was revealed on the day of Arafah, with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, standing at Arafah. Sufyan said: And I doubt whether Friday or not - meaning: Today I have perfected for you your religion, completed upon you My favor, and approved for you Islam as religion."
"273","Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab, from Abu Musa, saying: I came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, while he was at al-Batha. He said: With what did you enter consecration? I said: With a consecration like the consecration of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. He said: Did you drive sacrificial animals? I said: No. He said: Circumambulate the House and between al-Safa and al-Marwah, then release consecration. I circumambulated the House and between al-Safa and al-Marwah, then came to a woman of my people, who combed me and washed my head. I used to give people rulings to that effect in the leadership of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and the leadership of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him. While I was standing in the season of pilgrimage, a man came to me and said: You do not know what the Commander of the Believers has newly decided concerning rites. I said: O people, whoever we gave a ruling, this Commander of the Believers is coming to you, so follow him in it. When he came I said: What is this you have newly decided concerning rites? He said: If we take by the Book of Allah the Exalted, Allah the Exalted said: And complete pilgrimage and lesser pilgrimage for Allah. And if we take by the practice of our Prophet, he did not release consecration until he sacrificed the offering."
"274","Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Ibrahim ibn Abd al-Ala, from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, saying: I saw Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, kissing the Stone and saying: I know you are a stone that neither harms nor benefits, but I saw Abu al-Qasim, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, fond of you."
"275","Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Sufyan - and Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Sufyan informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Maymun, saying: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said - Abd al-Razzaq said: I heard Umar, may Allah be pleased with him - The polytheists would not pour forth from the crowded place until the sun rose upon Thabir. Abd al-Razzaq said: They used to say: Let Thabir shine, that we may hasten. Meaning: So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, opposed them and poured forth before the sun rose."
"276","Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Malik narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Abbas, saying: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Allah the Exalted sent Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and revealed to him the Book. Among what was revealed to him was the verse of stoning. We recited it, reasoned it, and retained it. I fear that with length of time people will say: We do not find the verse of stoning, so an obligation Allah the Exalted revealed is abandoned. Stoning in the Book of Allah the Exalted is a truth upon whoever commits adultery when married, of men and women, when proof is established, or pregnancy, or confession."
"277","Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Malik, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: I heard Hisham ibn Hakim reciting the chapter of al-Furqan in prayer otherwise than I recite it, though the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, had taught it to me. I seized his garment and took him to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: O Messenger of Allah, I heard him reciting the chapter of al-Furqan otherwise than you taught me. He said: Recite. He recited the recitation I had heard from him, and he said: Thus it was revealed. Then he said to me: Recite. I recited, and he said: Thus it was revealed. This Quran was revealed in seven modes, so recite what is easy of it."
"278","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari, that they heard Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: I passed by Hisham ibn Hakim ibn Hizam reciting the chapter of al-Furqan, and he mentioned its meaning."
"279","Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from al-Saib ibn Yazid, from Abdullah ibn al-Sadi, saying: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to me: Was I not told that you undertake works of the people, and when you are given the wage you do not accept it? He said: Yes. He said: What do you intend by that? He said: I am rich; I have slaves and horses. I wish my work to be charity upon the Muslims. He said: Do not do it, for I used to do the like of what you do: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, would give me the gift, and I would say: Give it to one poorer than me. He said: Take it; either enrich yourself by it or give it in charity. Whatever Allah brings you of this wealth, without your aspiring to it or asking for it, take it. Whatever is not, do not pursue it with your soul."
"280","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Mamar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from al-Saib ibn Yazid, saying: Umar met Abdullah ibn al-Sadi and mentioned its meaning, except that he said: Give it in charity. And he said: Do not pursue it with your soul."
"281","Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Malik, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: I gave a horse to be ridden in the way of Allah, and its owner neglected it. I wanted to buy it, thinking he would sell it cheap. I said: Until I ask the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. He said: Do not buy it, even if he gives it to you for a dirham, for one who takes back his charity is like the dog returning to its vomit."
"282","He said: I read to Abd al-Rahman, from Malik, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Ubayd, freedman of Ibn Azhar, that he said: I witnessed the festival with Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him. He came, prayed, then departed and addressed the people, saying: These two are days the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade fasting: the day of your breaking fast from your fasting, and the other a day in which you eat from your sacrifices."
"283","Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Ishaq, from Salim ibn Abdullah, saying: Umar was a jealous man. When he went out to prayer, Atikah daughter of Zayd would follow him. He disliked her going out, yet disliked preventing her. He used to relate that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: When your women seek your permission for prayer, do not prevent them."
"284","Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Malik, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar, saying: Were it not for the latter of the Muslims, no town would be opened but I would divide it as the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, divided Khaybar."
"285","Ismail narrated to us, Salamah ibn Alqamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, saying: I was told of Abu al-Ajfa al-Sulami, saying: I heard Umar saying: Do not be excessive in the dowries of women. Do not be excessive in the dowries of women, for had it been an honor in this world or piety with Allah, the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, would have been most entitled to it among you. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, did not dower any of his wives, nor was any of his daughters dowered, more than twelve ounces. A man is tried by his wife's dowry - and once he said: A man is excessive in his wife's dowry - until it becomes enmity in himself, until he says: I was burdened for you with the suspending strap of the water-skin. He said: I was an Arab-born youth and did not know what the suspending strap of the water-skin is. He said: And another you say for one killed in your campaigns and dead: So-and-so was killed a martyr, and so-and-so died a martyr. Perhaps he loaded the hinder part of his mount or the hump of his riding camel with gold or silver, seeking trade. Do not say that, but say as the Prophet said - or as Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever is killed or dies in the way of Allah is in Paradise."
"286","Ismail narrated to us, al-Jurayri Said informed us, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Firas, saying: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, addressed, saying: O people, we knew you when the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was among us, and revelation descended, and Allah informed us of your tidings. Now the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, has departed, and revelation has ceased, and we know you only by what we say to you: whoever of you displays good, we think good of him and love him for it, and whoever of you displays evil to us, we think evil of him and hate him for it. Your secrets are between you and your Lord. A time came upon me when I thought whoever recited the Quran sought Allah and what is with Him, then it appeared to me latterly that men recite it seeking what is with people. Seek Allah by your recitation, and seek Him by your deeds. By Allah, I do not send my workers to you to strike your skins or take your wealth, but I send them to you to teach you your religion and your practice. Whoever has something done to him other than that, let him raise it to me, for by Him in whose hand is my soul, I shall surely retaliate for him from him. Amr ibn al-As leapt up and said: O Commander of the Believers, what if a man of the Muslims is over subjects and disciplines one of his subjects - will you retaliate from him? He said: Yes, by Him in whose hand is the soul of Umar, I shall surely retaliate from him. I saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, retaliate from himself. Do not strike the Muslims lest you humiliate them, nor keep them on campaigns lest you tempt them, nor withhold their rights lest you drive them to disbelief, nor settle them in thickets lest you waste them."
"287","Ismail narrated to us another time, Salamah ibn Alqamah informed us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, saying: I was told of Abu al-Ajfa, saying: I heard Umar saying: Do not be excessive in the dowries of women, and he mentioned the account. Ismail said: Ayyub and Hisham and Ibn Awn mentioned, from Muhammad, from Abu al-Ajfa, from Umar, the like of the account of Salamah, except that they said: Muhammad did not say: I was told of Abu al-Ajfa."
"288","Ismail narrated to us, Ayyub narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Abi Mulaykah, saying: I was with Abdullah ibn Umar while we awaited the funeral of Umm Aban daughter of Uthman ibn Affan, with Amr ibn Uthman beside him. Ibn Abbas came, led by his guide. He said: I think he told him of the place of Ibn Umar, so he came and sat beside me, with me between them. A sound came from the house, so Ibn Umar said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: The dead is punished for the weeping of his family over him. Abdullah stated it unrestricted. Ibn Abbas said: We were with the Commander of the Believers Umar until, when we were at al-Bayda, he saw a man alighting in the shade of a tree. He said to me: Go and learn who that is. I went, and behold it was Suhayb. I returned to him and said: You commanded me to learn for you who that is, and it is Suhayb. He said: Command him to join us. I said: He has his family with him. He said: Even if he has his family with him - and Ayyub once perhaps said: To join us. When we reached Medina, the Commander of the Believers had not long remained before he was struck. Suhayb came and said: O my brother, O my companion. Umar said: Did you not know, or did you not hear, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The dead is punished for some of the weeping of his family over him. As for Abdullah, he stated it unrestricted, while Umar said: For some of the weeping. I came to Aishah and mentioned to her the saying of Umar, and she said: No, by Allah, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, did not say the dead is punished for the weeping of anyone, but the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Allah, mighty and majestic, increases the disbeliever in punishment for the weeping of his family, and Allah is He who makes laugh and makes weep, and no bearer of burdens bears another's burden. Ayyub said: Ibn Abi Mulaykah said: al-Qasim narrated to me, saying: When the saying of Umar and Ibn Umar reached Aishah, she said: You relate to me from neither liars nor those lied about, but hearing errs."
"289","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj informed us, Abdullah ibn Abi Mulaykah informed me, and he mentioned the meaning of the account of Ayyub, except that he said: Ibn Umar said to Amr ibn Uthman, facing him: Will you not forbid weeping, for the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The dead is punished for the weeping of his family over him."
"290","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj informed us, Abdullah ibn Abi Mulaykah informed me, saying: A daughter of Uthman ibn Affan died in Mecca, attended by Ibn Umar and Ibn Abbas, with me sitting between them. Ibn Umar said to Amr ibn Uthman, facing him: Will you not forbid weeping, for the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The dead is punished for the weeping of his family over him. And he mentioned the like of the account of Ismail, from Ayyub, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah."
"291","Husayn ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Israil narrated to us, from Simak, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, saying: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I was in a company traveling on a campaign with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and I swore, saying: No, by my father. A man behind me called out: Do not swear by your fathers. I turned, and behold it was the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"292","Muhammad ibn Musayyar Abu Sad al-Saghani narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ata, from Malik ibn Aws ibn al-Hadathan, saying: Umar used to swear three oaths, saying: By Allah, no one is more entitled to this wealth than another, nor am I more entitled to it than another. By Allah, there is no Muslim but he has a share in this wealth, except a slave owned. But we are according to our stations in the Book of Allah the Exalted and our allotment from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: a man and his tribulation in Islam, a man and his precedence in Islam, a man and his usefulness in Islam, and a man and his need. By Allah, if I remain for them, the shepherd at the mountain of Sanaa shall surely receive his share of this wealth while pasturing in his place."
"293","Abd al-Quddus ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, Safwan narrated to us, Abu al-Mukhariq Zuhayr ibn Salim narrated to me, that Umayr ibn Sad al-Ansari - Umar had appointed him over Hims - and he mentioned the account. Umar said, meaning to Kab: I ask you about a matter, so do not conceal from me. He said: By Allah, I shall not conceal from you anything I know. He said: What is the most fearful thing you fear for the community of Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him? He said: Misguiding leaders. Umar said: You spoke truly; he confided that to me and informed me of it, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"294","Yaqub narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Salih: Ibn Shihab said: Salim said: I heard Abdullah ibn Umar saying: Umar said: Send to me a physician to look at this wound of mine. He said: They sent to an Arab physician, and he gave Umar nabidh to drink, and the nabidh resembled blood when it came out of the stab beneath the navel. He said: I called another physician from the Helpers, from Banu Muawiyah, and he gave him milk to drink, and the milk came out of the stab thick and white. The physician said to him: O Commander of the Believers, make your bequest. Umar said: The brother of Banu Muawiyah told me truly; had you said otherwise, I would have belied you. He said: The people wept over him when they heard that. He said: Do not weep over us; whoever would weep, let him go out. Did you not hear what the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said? He said: The dead is punished for the weeping of his family over him. For that reason Abdullah would not approve weeping beside him over any dead one of his children or others."
"295","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, al-Thawri informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Maymun, saying: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: The people of pre-Islamic ignorance would not pour forth from the crowded place until they saw the sun upon Thabir, and they used to say: Let Thabir shine, that we may hasten. So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, poured forth before sunrise."
"296","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari, that they heard Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: I passed by Hisham ibn Hakim ibn Hizam reciting the chapter of al-Furqan in the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. I listened to his recitation, and he was reciting in many modes I had not been taught by the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. I nearly attacked him in prayer, but waited until he finished. When he finished, I seized him by his cloak and said: Who taught you this chapter you recite? He said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, taught it to me. He said: I said to him: You lie, for by Allah the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, taught me this chapter you recite. He said: I set out leading him to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: O Messenger of Allah, I heard this one reciting the chapter of al-Furqan in modes you did not teach me, though you taught me the chapter of al-Furqan. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Release him, O Umar. Recite, O Hisham. He recited to him the recitation I had heard him recite, and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Thus it was revealed. Then the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Recite, O Umar. I recited the recitation the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, had taught me, and he said: Thus it was revealed. Then the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The Quran was revealed in seven modes, so recite of it what is easy."
"297","Al-Hakam ibn Nafi narrated to us, Shuayb informed us, from al-Zuhri: Urwah narrated to me, from the account of al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari, that they heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: I heard Hisham ibn Hakim ibn Hizam reciting the chapter of al-Furqan in the lifetime of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. I listened to his recitation, and he was reciting in many modes the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, had not taught me. I nearly attacked him in prayer, but waited until he finished. When he finished, he mentioned its meaning."
"298","Husayn ibn Ali narrated to us, from Zaidah, from Asim, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, saying: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever of you seeks the Night of Decree, let him seek it in the last ten, odd-numbered."
"299","Muhammad ibn Bishr narrated to us, Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to us, from his father, from Ibn Umar, that Umar was told: Will you not appoint a successor? He said: If I leave it, one better than me left it, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. And if I appoint a successor, one better than me appointed a successor, Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him."
"300","Yazid narrated to us, Yahya ibn Said informed us, that Muhammad ibn Ibrahim informed him that he heard Alqamah ibn Waqqas al-Laythi saying that he heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, addressing the people, saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: Deeds are only by intentions, and to each man only what he intended. Whoever's emigration was to Allah and to His Messenger, his emigration is to Allah and to His Messenger. Whoever's emigration was to a worldly gain he attains or a woman he marries, his emigration is to what he emigrated to."
"301","Yazid narrated to us, Asim narrated to us, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: Wear the izar and the rida and sandals, and cast off the khuffs and trousers, and cast off knees, and leap with a leap, and keep to the Maaddite way, and shoot at targets, and leave luxury and the dress of the Persians, and beware of silk, for the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade it and said: Do not wear silk except like this, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, pointed with his two fingers."
"302","Yazid narrated to us, Yahya informed us, from Said ibn al-Musayyab, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Beware lest you perish concerning the verse of stoning, and that someone should say: We do not find two hadd punishments in the Book of Allah Almighty, for I saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, stone, and we stoned after him."
"303","Yazid narrated to us, al-Awwam informed us, a shaykh who was stationed at the coast narrated to me, he said: I met Abu Salih, the freedman of Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us, from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, that he said: There is no night but the sea overlooks the land three times therein, seeking permission from Allah to flood them, but Allah, Mighty and Majestic, restrains it."
"304","Yazid narrated to us, Abd al-Malik informed us, from Anas ibn Sirin, he said: I said to Ibn Umar: Tell me about your divorcing your wife. He said: I divorced her while she was menstruating. He said: I mentioned that to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and he mentioned it to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Order him to take her back, and when she becomes pure, let him divorce her in her purity. He said: I said to him: Did you count the one you divorced while menstruating. He said: Why should I not count it, even if I was incapable and foolish."
"305","Yazid narrated to us, Asbagh informed us, from Abu al-Ala al-Shami, he said: Abu Umama wore a new garment, and when it reached his collarbone he said: Praise be to Allah who clothed me with what I cover my nakedness and beautify myself with in my life. Then he said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, say: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever gets a new garment and wears it, and says when it reaches his collarbone: Praise be to Allah who clothed me with what I cover my nakedness and beautify myself with in my life, then turns to the garment he wore out, or he said cast off, and gives it in charity, he will be in the protection of Allah Almighty and in the neighborhood of Allah and in the shelter of Allah, alive and dead, alive and dead, alive and dead."
"306","Yazid narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I asked the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, I said: O Messenger of Allah, if one of us wants to sleep while junub, what should he do before ghusl. He said: He should perform wudu as for prayer, then sleep."
"307","Yazid narrated to us, Warqa informed us, and Abu al-Nadr said: Warqa narrated to us, from Abd al-Ala al-Thalabi, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, he said: I was with al-Bara ibn Azib, and Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, in al-Baqi looking at the crescent, and a rider came, and Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, met him and said: From where have you come. He said: From the Arabs. He said: Did you sight the crescent. He said: Yes. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Allah is Greatest, one man suffices the Muslims. Then Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, stood and performed wudu and wiped over his khuffs, then prayed Maghrib, then said: Thus I saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, do. Abu al-Nadr said: He was wearing a jubba with narrow sleeves, so he took his hand out from under it and wiped."
"308","Yazid narrated to us, Jarir informed us, al-Zubayr ibn al-Khirrit informed us, from Abu Labid, he said: A man from Tahiya emigrated, called Bayrah ibn Asad, and he came to Medina days after the death of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, saw him and knew he was a stranger, and said to him: Who are you. He said: From the people of Oman. He said: Yes. He said: He took his hand and brought him to Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: This is from the people of the land I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: I know a land called Oman, the sea borders its side, with a tribe of Arabs, if my messenger came to them they would not shoot him with an arrow nor a stone."
"309","Yazid narrated to us, Asim ibn Muhammad informed us, from his father, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, he said, I know it only as marfu: Allah, Blessed and Almighty, says: Whoever humbles himself to Me like this, and Yazid turned the inside of his palm to the earth and brought it near the earth, I will raise him like this, and he turned the inside of his palm to the heaven and raised it toward the heaven."
"310","Yazid narrated to us, Daylam ibn Ghazwan al-Abdi informed us, Maymun al-Kurdi narrated to us, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, he said: I was sitting beneath the minbar of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, while he was addressing the people, and he said in his sermon: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: What I fear most for this ummah is every hypocrite eloquent of tongue."
"311","Rawh narrated to us, Malik narrated to us, and Ishaq narrated to us, Malik informed me, Abu Abd al-Rahman Abdullah ibn Ahmad said, and Musab al-Zubayri narrated to us, Malik narrated to me, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysa, that Abd al-Hamid ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, informed him, from Muslim ibn Yasar al-Juhani, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, was asked about this verse: And when your Lord took from the children of Adam, from their backs, their descendants, the verse, and Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, asked about it, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Allah created Adam, then wiped his back with His right hand and brought forth from him offspring, and said: I created these for Paradise, and they will do the deeds of the people of Paradise. Then He wiped his back and brought forth from him offspring, and said: I created these for the Fire, and they will do the deeds of the people of the Fire. A man said: O Messenger of Allah, then what is deeds for. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: When Allah, Mighty and Majestic, creates the servant for Paradise, He employs him in the deeds of the people of Paradise until he dies upon a deed of the deeds of the people of Paradise, and admits him by it to Paradise. And when He creates the servant for the Fire, He employs him in the deeds of the people of the Fire until he dies upon a deed of the deeds of the people of the Fire, and admits him by it to the Fire."
"312","Rawh narrated to us, Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim ibn Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, from his father, that a man from the companions of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, entered the mosque on Friday while Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, was standing addressing, and Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: What hour is this. He said: O Commander of the Faithful, I returned from the market and heard the call, and I did no more than perform wudu, then I came. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Wudu also, when you knew that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to order us to perform ghusl."
"313","Rawh narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Atiq informed me, from Abdullah ibn Babayh, from some of the Banu Yala, from Yala ibn Umayya, he said: I performed tawaf with Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and he touched the corner. Yala said: I was on the side toward the House, and when I reached the western corner which is next to the Black, I pulled his hand for him to touch, and he said: What is the matter with you. I said: Will you not touch. He said: Did you not perform tawaf with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. I said: Yes. He said: Did you see him touch these two western corners. He said: I said: No. He said: Is there not for you in him a good example. He said: I said: Yes. He said: Then move away from you."
"314","Uthman ibn Umar and Abu Amir narrated to us, they said: Malik narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Malik ibn Aws ibn al-Hadathan, he said: I came with dinars of mine and wanted to exchange them, and Talha ibn Ubaydullah met me and exchanged them and took them, and said: Until my treasurer Salm comes, Abu Amir said: From al-Ghaba, and he said in all of them: Hand to hand. He said: I asked Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, about that, and he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: Gold for silver is riba except hand to hand, and wheat for wheat is riba except hand to hand, and barley for barley is riba except hand to hand, and dates for dates is riba except hand to hand."
"315","Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, Yunus informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Said ibn al-Musayyab, that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The dead is punished by the weeping of his family over him."
"316","Bakr ibn Isa narrated to us, Abu Awana narrated to us, from al-Mughira, from al-Shabi, from Adi ibn Hatim, he said: I came to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, among people of my tribe, and he began assigning to a man of Tayy two thousand, and turning away from me. He said: I faced him and he turned away from me, then I came to him from before his face and he turned away from me. He said: I said: O Commander of the Faithful, do you know me. He said: He laughed until he lay back, then said: Yes, by Allah, I know you. You believed when they disbelieved, and came forward when they turned back, and fulfilled when they betrayed, and the first charity that whitened the face of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and the faces of his companions was the charity of Tayy you brought to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. Then he began to excuse himself, then said: I assigned stipends to people whom poverty had afflicted, and they are chiefs of their clans, for the obligations that befall them."
"317","Abd al-Malik ibn Amr narrated to us, Hisham ibn Sad narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, he said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, say: What is this trotting now and uncovering the shoulders, when Allah has established Islam and removed disbelief and its people, yet we do not leave anything we used to do at the time of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"318","Abd al-Samad and Affan narrated to us, they said: Dawud ibn Abi al-Furat narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Burayda narrated to us, Affan said: From Ibn Burayda, from Abu al-Aswad al-Dili, he said: I came to Medina when sickness had fallen upon it, Abd al-Samad said: They were dying a severe death, and I sat with Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and a funeral passed by him and its owner was praised with good, and Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: It is due. Then another passed and its owner was praised with good, and he said: It is due. Then another passed and it was praised with evil, and Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: It is due. Abu al-Aswad said: I said to him: O Commander of the Faithful, what is due. He said: I said as the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Any Muslim for whom four testify with good, Allah admits him to Paradise. He said: We said: And three. He said: And three. We said: And two. He said: And two. He said: And we did not ask him about one."
"319","Abd al-Samad narrated to us, Harb, meaning Ibn Shaddad, narrated to us, Yahya narrated to us, Abu Salama narrated to us, Abu Hurayra narrated to us, he said: While Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, was addressing, a man came and sat, and Umar said: Why do you delay from Friday. The man said: O Commander of the Faithful, it was only that I heard the call and performed wudu, then I came. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: And also, did you not hear the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: When one of you goes to Friday, let him perform ghusl."
"320","Abd al-Samad narrated to us, my father narrated to me, al-Husayn al-Muallim narrated to us, Yahya narrated to us, Abu Salama informed me, that Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, informed him, that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, while he was addressing, and he mentioned it."
"321","Abd al-Samad narrated to us, Harb narrated to us, Yahya narrated to us, from Imran ibn Hittan, as Harb thought, that he asked Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, about wearing silk, and he said: Ask Aisha about it. So he asked Aisha, and she said: Ask Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him. So he asked Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and he said: Abu Hafs narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever wears silk in this world will have no share of it in the Hereafter."
"322","Yahya ibn Hammad and Affan narrated to us, they said: Abu Awana narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Abdullah al-Awdi, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Himyari, Ibn Abbas narrated to us at Basra, he said: I was the first who came to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, when he was stabbed, and he said: Remember three things from me, for I fear people will not reach me. As for me, I did not decide a ruling concerning kalala, and I did not appoint a successor over the people, and every slave of his is freed. The people said to him: Appoint a successor. He said: Whichever of those I do, one better than me did it. If I leave the people to decide their affair, the Prophet of Allah, upon him prayer and peace, left it. And if I appoint a successor, one better than me appointed a successor, Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him. I said to him: Rejoice with Paradise. You accompanied the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and prolonged his companionship, and you were given charge of the affairs of the believers, and you were strong and discharged the trust. He said: As for your giving me glad tidings of Paradise, by Allah, if I had, Affan said: No, by Allah besides whom there is no god, if I had the world with what is in it, I would ransom myself with it from the terror before me, before I know the news. As for what you said about the affair of the believers, by Allah, I wish that were balanced, neither for me nor against me. As for what you mentioned of companionship of the Prophet of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, that is it."
"323","Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ayyash, from Hakim ibn Hakim, from Abu Umama ibn Sahl, he said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to Abu Ubayda ibn al-Jarrah: Teach your boys swimming, and your fighters shooting. They used to go to targets, and a stray arrow came to a boy and killed him, and no origin was found for it, and he was in the care of a maternal uncle of his. Abu Ubayda wrote about him to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him: To whom do I pay his blood money. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to him: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to say: Allah and His Messenger are the patron of one who has no patron, and the maternal uncle is heir of one who has no heir."
"324","Abdullah ibn Zayd narrated to us, Ibn Lahia informed us, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: The wala is inherited by whoever inherits wealth, from father or son."
"325","Muhammad ibn Ubayd narrated to us, al-Amash narrated to us, from Ibrahim, from Abis ibn Rabia, he said: I saw Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, come to the Stone and say: By Allah, I know you are a stone that neither harms nor benefits, and were it not that I saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, kiss you, I would not kiss you. Then he drew near and kissed it."
"326","Abu Said narrated to us, Dujayn Abu al-Ghusn, a Basran, narrated to us, he said: I came to Medina and met Aslam, the freedman of Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: Narrate to me from Umar. He said: I cannot, I fear to add or omit. When we said to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him: Narrate to us from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he said: I fear to add a letter or omit. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever lies upon me is in the Fire."
"327","Abu Said narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, freedman of the family of al-Zubayr, from Salim, from his father, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever says in a market: There is no god but Allah alone, with no partner, to Him belongs dominion and to Him belongs praise, in His hand is good, He gives life and causes death, and He is over all things Powerful, Allah writes for him by it a million good deeds, and erases from him by it a million evil deeds, and builds for him a house in Paradise."
"328","Abu Said narrated to us, Ikrima ibn Ammar narrated to us, Abu Zumayl narrated to us, Ibn Abbas narrated to me, Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me, he said: On the day of Khaybar a group of the companions of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came saying: So-and-so is a martyr and so-and-so is a martyr, until they passed by a man and said: So-and-so is a martyr. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: No, I saw him dragged to the Fire in a cloak he stole. Go out, O Umar, and call among the people: Only the believers enter Paradise. So I went out and called: Only the believers enter Paradise."
"329","Abu Said narrated to us, Israil narrated to us, Said ibn Masruq narrated to us, from Sad ibn Ubayda, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, from Umar, that he said: No, by my father. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Stop, whoever swears by something besides Allah has committed shirk."
"330","Hammad al-Khayyat narrated to us, Abdullah narrated to us, from Nafi, that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, added to the mosque from the pillar to the maqsura, and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, added, and Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Were it not that I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: We should add to our mosque, I would not have added to it."
"331","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Mamar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba ibn Masud, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, with the truth and sent down with him the Book, and among what was sent down upon him was the verse of stoning, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, stoned, and we stoned after him. Then he said: We used to recite: Do not turn away from your fathers, for it is disbelief in you, or: It is disbelief in you to turn away from your fathers. Then the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Do not extol me as the son of Maryam was extolled, I am only a servant, so say: His servant and His Messenger. And perhaps Mamar said: As the Christians extolled the son of Maryam."
"332","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Mamar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him: I heard the people say a saying, and I swore I would say it to you. They claim you will not appoint a successor. He put his head down for a while, then raised it and said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, preserves His religion. If I do not appoint a successor, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, did not appoint a successor. And if I appoint a successor, Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, appointed a successor. He said: By Allah, it was only that he mentioned the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and Abu Bakr, and I knew he would not equate anyone with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and that he would not appoint a successor."
"333","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Mamar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Malik ibn Aws ibn al-Hadathan, he said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, sent for me, and he mentioned the hadith, and I said to you both: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: We are not inherited, what we leave is charity."
"334","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Mamar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyab, he said: When Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, died, he was wept over, and Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The dead is punished by the weeping of the living."
"335","Ibrahim ibn Khalid narrated to us, Rabah narrated to us, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, died and those who disbelieved disbelieved, Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: O Abu Bakr, how do you fight the people when the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: I was ordered to fight the people until they say: There is no god but Allah. Whoever says: There is no god but Allah, has protected from me his wealth and himself, and his reckoning is upon Allah, Mighty and Majestic. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I will fight whoever separates between prayer and zakat. Zakat is the right of wealth. By Allah, if they withheld from me a young goat they used to give to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, I would fight them for withholding it. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: By Allah, it was only that I saw Allah had opened the breast of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, to fighting, and I knew it was the truth."
"336","Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from al-Zuhri, from Malik ibn Aws, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: We are not inherited, what we leave is charity."
"337","Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from al-Zuhri, from Malik ibn Aws, he said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, sent for me, and he mentioned the hadith, and said: The wealth of Banu al-Nadir was from what Allah granted to His Messenger from what the Muslims did not press with horse nor camel, and he used to spend upon his family from it a years maintenance, and what remained he put into horses and weapons as preparation in the way of Allah, Mighty and Majestic."
"338","Sufyan narrated to us, from Hisham, from his father, from Asim ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, from his father, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: When night comes and day departs and the sun sets, the fasting person has broken his fast."
"339","Sufyan narrated to us, from Yahya, meaning Ibn Said, from Ubayd ibn Hunayn, from Ibn Abbas, he said: I wanted to ask Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and I saw no opportunity for two years. When we were at Marr al-Zahran and he went to relieve himself, and came after relieving himself, I went to pour water for him from the water, I said: O Commander of the Faithful, who are the two women who backed one another against the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. He said: Aisha and Hafsa, may Allah be pleased with them."
"340","Sufyan narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he heard it from Abu al-Ajfa: I heard Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, say: Do not be excessive in the dowries of women, for had it been an honor in this world or piety in the Hereafter, the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, would have been most entitled to it among you. He did not marry any of his daughters nor his wives for more than twelve uqiyya. And another thing you say in your expeditions: So-and-so was killed a martyr, so-and-so died a martyr, and perhaps he loaded the back of his beast or stuffed his mount with gold and silver seeking trade. Do not say that, but say as Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever is killed in the way of Allah is in Paradise."
"341","Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, Said ibn Abi Aruba narrated to us, he dictated it to me, from Qatada, from Salim ibn Abi al-Jad al-Ghatafani, from Madan ibn Abi Talha al-Yamari, that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, stood as a preacher, and praised Allah and extolled Him, and mentioned the Prophet of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, then said: I saw a dream as if a rooster pecked me twice, and I see that only as the approach of my term. People order me to appoint a successor, yet Allah, Mighty and Majestic, would not neglect His caliphate and His religion, nor what He sent His Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, with. If a matter hastens me, the caliphate is consultation among these six men with whom the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, died while pleased with them. Whomever of them you pledge to, listen to him and obey. I have known that men will attack this affair, and I fought them with this hand of mine for Islam. If they do, they are the enemies of Allah, the disbelievers, the misguided. By Allah, I do not leave behind me anything more important to me than the matter of kalala. I asked the Prophet of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about it, and he was never harsher to me in anything than he was in it, until he poked with his hand or his finger in my chest or my side and said: O Umar, the verse revealed in summer at the end of Surat al-Nisa suffices you. If I live, I will decide in it a decision over which no one who reads the Quran or does not read the Quran will differ. Then he said: O Allah, I call You to witness over the governors of the cities, for I sent them to teach the people their religion and the sunna of their Prophet, and to divide among them their fay, and to be just over them, and what is unclear to them they raise to me. Then he said: O people, you eat from two trees I see only as evil, this garlic and onion. I saw a man at the time of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, whose smell was found from him, taken by his hand until taken out to al-Baqi. Whoever must eat them, let him kill them by cooking. He said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, preached it on Friday, and he was struck on Wednesday, with four nights remaining of Dhu al-Hijja."
"342","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Hushaym informed me, from al-Hajjaj ibn Artat, from al-Hakam ibn Utayba, from Umara, from Abu Burda, from Abu Musa, that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: It is the sunna of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, meaning muta, but I fear they will consummate with them under the arak trees, then go with them as pilgrims."
"343","Ali ibn Asim narrated to us, Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad informed us, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah, from his father or his grandfather, Yazid is the doubter, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, perform wudu after hadath and wipe over his khuffs and pray."
"344","Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, Shuba narrated to us, from Simak, he said: I heard Iyad al-Ashari, he said: I witnessed Yarmuk, and over us were five commanders: Abu Ubayda ibn al-Jarrah, Yazid ibn Abi Sufyan, Ibn Hasana, Khalid ibn al-Walid, and Iyad, and this Iyad is not the one who narrated to Simak. He said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: When there is fighting, Abu Ubayda is over you. He said: We wrote to him that death had surged toward us, and we sought reinforcement. He wrote to us: Your letter has come to me seeking reinforcement from me, and I direct you to one mightier in aid and more present in soldiers, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, so seek His aid. Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was aided on the day of Badr with fewer than your number. When this letter of mine comes to you, fight them and do not refer back to me. He said: We fought them and routed them and killed them for four farsakhs. He said: We took wealth and consulted, and Iyad advised us to give for every head ten. He said: Abu Ubayda said: Who will race me. A youth said: I will, if you do not get angry. He said: He outran him, and I saw the two braids of Abu Ubayda bouncing behind him upon an Arabian horse."
"345","Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, Uyayna informed us, from Ali ibn Zayd, he said: I came to Medina and entered upon Salim ibn Abdullah, wearing a jubba of khazz, and Salim said to me: What are you doing with these clothes. I heard my father narrate from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Only one who has no share wears silk."
"346","Abu al-Mundhir Ismail ibn Umar narrated to us, I think from Hajjaj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: A man deliberately killed his son, and he was brought to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and he imposed one hundred camels: thirty hiqqa, thirty jadhaa, and forty thaniyya. And he said: The killer does not inherit. Were it not that I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: A father is not killed for a son, I would have killed you."
"347","Hushaym and Yazid narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Said, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Were it not that I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: There is nothing for a killer, I would have given you inheritance. He said: He called the maternal uncle of the slain and gave him the camels."
"348","Yaqub narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, Abdullah ibn Abi Najih and Amr ibn Shuayb narrated to me, both from Mujahid ibn Jabr, and he mentioned the hadith, and said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, took from the camels thirty hiqqa, thirty jadhaa, and forty thaniyya up to a bazil of its year, all pregnant. He said: Then he called the brother of the slain and gave them to him to the exclusion of his father, and said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: There is nothing for a killer."
"349","Ismail narrated to us, Ayyub narrated to us, from Ikrima ibn Khalid, from Malik ibn Aws ibn al-Hadathan, he said: al-Abbas and Ali, upon them peace, came to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, disputing, and al-Abbas said: Judge between me and this, such and such. The people said: Decide between them, decide between them. He said: I will not decide between them. They knew that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: We are not inherited, what we leave is charity."
"350","Ismail narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Aruba, from Qatada, from Ibn al-Musayyab, that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Among the last revealed is the verse of riba, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, died without explaining it, so leave riba and doubt."
"351","Abu Abdullah Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, Shuba narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Umara ibn Umayr, from Ibrahim ibn Abi Musa, from Abu Musa, that he used to give fatwa for muta, and a man said to him: Go slow with some of your fatwas, for you do not know what the Commander of the Faithful has innovated concerning rites after you, until he met him after and asked him. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I knew the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, did it and his companions, but I disliked that they remain with them consummating in the arak trees and go to hajj with their heads dripping."
"352","Muhammad ibn Jafar and Hajjaj narrated to us, they said: Shuba narrated to us, from Sad ibn Ibrahim, he said: I heard Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba narrate from Ibn Abbas, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, performed hajj and wanted to address the people, and Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf said: The riffraff of the people have gathered with you, so delay that until you come to Medina. When he came to Medina, I drew near him close to the minbar and heard him say: People say: What is stoning, when in the Book of Allah is only flogging. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, stoned, and we stoned after him. Were it not that they would say: He affirmed in the Book of Allah what is not in it, I would have affirmed it as it was revealed."
"353","Muhammad ibn Jafar and Hajjaj narrated to us, they said: Shuba narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, he said: I heard al-Numan, meaning Ibn Bashir, addressing, he said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, mentioned what people attained of the world, and said: I saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, spend the day writhing, finding not poor dates to fill his belly."
"354","Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, Shuba narrated to us, and Hajjaj said: Shuba narrated to me, he said: I heard Qatada narrate from Said ibn al-Musayyab, from Ibn Umar, from his father, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he said: The dead is punished in his grave for what is wailed over him. And Hajjaj said: For wailing over him."
"355","Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, Shuba narrated to us, from Qatada, he said: I heard Rufay Abu al-Aliya narrate from Ibn Abbas, men narrated to me, Shuba said: I think he said: From the companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he said: The most pleasing of them to me, Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade prayer at two times: after Asr until the sun sets, and after Subh until it rises."
"356","Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, Shuba narrated to us, and Hajjaj said: Shuba narrated to me, from Qatada, he said: I heard Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, he said: The letter of Umar came to us while we were in Adharbayjan with Utba ibn Farqad, or in Syria: Then, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade silk except like this, two fingers. Abu Uthman said: We saw only that it meant the marks."
"357","Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, Shuba narrated to us, and Hajjaj and Abu Dawud said: Shuba narrated to us, from Qatada, he said: I heard Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, he said: The letter of Umar came to us."
"358","Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, Shuba narrated to us, and Abu Dawud from Shuba, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Maymun, he said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, prayed Subh while at Jam, Abu Dawud said: We were with Umar at Jam, and he said: The polytheists used not to depart until the sun rose, saying: Shine, Thabir. The Prophet of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, opposed them and departed before sunrise."
"359","Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, Shuba narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, he said: I heard Ibn Umar say: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, asked the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: Janaba befalls me at night, what should I do. He said: Wash your member, then perform wudu, then sleep."
"360","Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, Shuba narrated to us, from Salama ibn Kuhayl, he said: I heard Abu al-Hakam, he said: I asked Ibn Umar about the jar, and he narrated to us from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade the jar, and the dubba, and the muzaffat."
"361","Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, Shuba narrated to us, from Asim al-Ahwal, from Abdullah ibn Sarjis, he said: I saw al-Usayli, meaning Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, kiss the Stone and say: I know you are a stone, but I saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, kiss you."
"362","Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, Shuba narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Jamra al-Dubai narrate from Juwayriya ibn Qudama, he said: I performed hajj and came to Medina in the year Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, was struck. He said: He addressed and said: I saw as if a red rooster pecked me once or twice, Shuba is the doubter. It happened that he was stabbed, and people were permitted to him. The first who entered upon him were the companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, then the people of Medina, then the people of Syria, then the people of Iraq were permitted, and I entered among those who entered. He said: Whenever people entered upon him they praised him and wept. He said: When we entered upon him, he had bound his belly with a black turban, and blood was flowing. He said: We said: Advise us. No one asked him for advice but us. He said: Upon you is the Book of Allah, for you will not go astray as long as you follow it. We said: Advise us. He said: I advise you concerning the Muhajirun, for people will increase and they will decrease. I advise you concerning the Ansar, for they are the stronghold of Islam to which refuge was taken. I advise you concerning the Bedouins, for they are your origin and your reinforcement. I advise you concerning the people of your dhimma, for they are the covenant of your Prophet and the provision of your dependents. Rise from me. He said: He added nothing for us beyond these words. Muhammad ibn Jafar said: Shuba said: Then I asked him after that, and he said concerning the Bedouins: I advise you concerning the Bedouins, for they are your brothers and the enemy of your enemy."
"363","Hajjaj narrated to us, Shuba informed us, I heard Abu Jamra al-Dubai narrate from Juwayriya ibn Qudama, he said: I performed hajj and came to Medina in the year Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, was struck. He said: He addressed and said: I saw as if a red rooster pecked me once or twice, Shuba is the doubter. He said: Only a Friday passed until he was stabbed, and he mentioned the like, except he said: I advise you concerning the people of your dhimma, for they are the dhimma of your Prophet. Shuba said: Then I asked him after that, and he said concerning the Bedouins: I advise you concerning the Bedouins, for they are your brothers and the enemy of your enemy."
"364","Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, Said narrated to us, and Abd al-Wahhab from Said, from Qatada, from Abu al-Aliya, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: Trustworthy men testified to me, among them Umar, and the most pleasing of them to me, Umar, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade prayer after Subh prayer until the sun rises, and after Asr until it sets."
"365","Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, Said narrated to us, from Qatada, from al-Shabi, from Suwayd ibn Ghafala, that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, addressed the people at al-Jabiya and said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade wearing silk except the place of two fingers, or three, or four, and he pointed with his palm."
"366","Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, Said narrated to us, from Qatada, from Said ibn al-Musayyab, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The dead is punished in his grave for what is wailed over him."
"367","Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, Kahmas narrated to us, from Ibn Burayda, and Yazid ibn Harun, Kahmas narrated to us, from Ibn Burayda, from Yahya ibn Yamar, he heard Ibn Umar, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me, he said: While we were one day with the Prophet of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, a man came upon us, intense in whiteness of clothes, intense in blackness of hair, Yazid said: We saw no trace of travel upon him, and none of us knew him, until he sat to the Prophet of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and rested his knees against his knees and placed his palms upon his thighs, then said: O Muhammad, inform me about Islam, what is Islam. He said: Islam is to testify that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, and to establish prayer, and give zakat, and fast Ramadan, and perform hajj to the House if you can find a way to it. He said: You spoke truthfully. He said: We were amazed at him, asking him and confirming him. He said: Then he said: Inform me about iman. He said: Iman is to believe in Allah and His angels and His books and His messengers and the Last Day and the decree, all of it, its good and its evil. He said: You spoke truthfully. He said: Inform me about ihsan, what is ihsan. Yazid said: To worship Allah as if you see Him, and if you do not see Him, He sees you. He said: Inform me about the Hour. He said: The one asked about it knows no more than the asker. He said: Inform me about its signs. He said: That the slave woman gives birth to her mistress, and that you see the barefoot, naked shepherds of sheep competing in building. He said: Then he went. He said: I remained a while, Yazid said: Three, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to me: O Umar, do you know who the asker was. He said: I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: It was Jibril, he came to teach you your religion."
"368","Abdullah ibn Yazid narrated to us, Kahmas narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Burayda, from Yahya ibn Yamar, he heard Ibn Umar, he said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us, he said: We were sitting with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he mentioned the hadith, except he said: No trace of travel was seen upon him. And he said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I remained three, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to me: O Umar."
"369","Bahz said, and Affan narrated to us, they said: Hammam narrated to us, Qatada narrated to us, from Abu Nadra, he said: I said to Jabir ibn Abdullah: Ibn al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him, forbids muta, and Ibn Abbas orders it. He said: He said to me: Upon my hand the hadith occurred. We performed muta with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, Affan said: And with Abu Bakr. When Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, took charge, he addressed the people and said: The Quran is the Quran, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, is the Messenger, and they were two mutas at the time of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, one of them the muta of hajj, and the other the muta of women."
"370","Hajjaj narrated to us, Ibn Lahia informed us, from Abdullah ibn Hubayra, from Abu Tamim, that he heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, say: I heard the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: If you relied upon Allah with true reliance, He would provide for you as He provides for birds, they go hungry and return full."
"371","Hajjaj narrated to us, Layth narrated to us, Bukayr ibn Abdullah narrated to me, from Busr ibn Said, from Ibn al-Saidi al-Maliki, that he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, employed me over sadaqa, and when I finished it and delivered it to him, he ordered wages for me. I said to him: I worked for Allah, and my reward is upon Allah. He said: Take what you are given, for I worked at the time of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he paid me wages, and I said like your saying, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to me: When you are given something without asking, eat and give charity."
"372","Hajjaj narrated to us, Layth narrated to us, Bukayr narrated to me, from Abd al-Malik ibn Said al-Ansari, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: I felt desire one day and kissed while fasting, and I came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: I did a grave thing today, I kissed while fasting. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: What if you rinsed with water while fasting. I said: There is no harm in that. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Then what."
"373","Yahya ibn Ishaq narrated to us, Ibn Lahia informed us, Abdullah ibn Hubayra narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Tamim al-Jayshani say: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, say: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: If you relied upon Allah with true reliance, He would provide for you as He provides for birds. Do you not see they go hungry and return full."
"374","Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Alqama ibn Marthad, from Sulayman ibn Burayda, from Ibn Yamar, he said: I said to Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him: We travel in the horizons and meet people who say: No qadar. Ibn Umar said: When you meet them, tell them Abdullah ibn Umar is quit of them, and they are quit of him, three times. Then he began to narrate: While we were with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, a man came, and he mentioned his appearance, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Come near. He came near, and said: Come near. He came near, and said: Come near. He came near until his knees almost touched his knees, and said: O Messenger of Allah, inform me what is iman, or about iman. He said: To believe in Allah and His angels and His books and His messengers and the Last Day, and to believe in the decree. Sufyan said: I think he said: Its good and its evil. He said: What is Islam. He said: Establishing prayer, and giving zakat, and hajj to the House, and fasting the month of Ramadan, and ghusl from janaba. All that he said: You spoke truthfully, you spoke truthfully. The people said: We never saw a man more reverent toward the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, than this, as if teaching the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. Then he said: O Messenger of Allah, inform me about ihsan. He said: To worship Allah, or worship Him, as if you see Him, and if you do not see Him, He sees you. All that we say: We never saw a man more reverent toward the Messenger of Allah than this, and he says: You spoke truthfully, you spoke truthfully. He said: Inform me about the Hour. He said: The one asked about it knows no more than the asker. He said: He said: You spoke truthfully. He said that repeatedly. We never saw a man more reverent toward the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, than this. Then he turned. Sufyan said: It reached me that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Seek him, and they did not find him. He said: This is Jibril, he came to teach you your religion. He never came to me in a form but I knew him, except this form."
"375","Abu Ahmad narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Alqama ibn Marthad, from Sulayman ibn Burayda, from Ibn Yamar, he said: I asked Ibn Umar, or a man asked him: We travel in this land and meet people who say: No qadar. Ibn Umar said: When you meet them, tell them Abdullah ibn Umar is quit of them, and they are quit of him. He said it three times. Then he began to narrate to us, he said: While we were with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, a man came and said: O Messenger of Allah, may I come near. He said: Come near. He came near a distance, then said: O Messenger of Allah, may I come near. He said: Come near. He came near a distance, then said: O Messenger of Allah, may I come near. He said: Come near. He came near a distance until his knees almost touched the knee of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: O Messenger of Allah, what is iman, and he mentioned its meaning."
"376","Hasan ibn Musa al-Ashyab narrated to us, Ibn Lahia narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Abi al-Walid narrated to us, from Uthman ibn Abdullah ibn Suraqa al-Adawi, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever shades the head of a warrior, Allah will shade him on the Day of Resurrection. Whoever equips a warrior until he departs with his equipment has the like of his reward. Whoever builds a mosque in which the name of Allah is mentioned, Allah builds for him a house in Paradise."
"377","Attab, meaning Ibn Ziyad, narrated to us, Abdullah, meaning Ibn al-Mubarak, narrated to us, Yunus informed us, from al-Zuhri, from al-Saib ibn Yazid and Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, Abdullah said: My father raised it to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he said: Whoever misses something of his wird, or he said: Of his portion of the night, and reads it between Fajr prayer and Zuhr, it is as if he read it in his night."
"378","Khalaf ibn al-Walid narrated to us, Israil narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Maysara, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: When the prohibition of wine came down, he said: O Allah, clarify to us concerning wine a healing clarification. This verse in Surat al-Baqara came down: They ask you about wine and gambling, say: In them is great sin. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, was called and it was recited to him, and he said: O Allah, clarify to us concerning wine a healing clarification. The verse in Surat al-Nisa came down: O you who believe, do not approach prayer while drunk. The caller of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, when he established prayer, called: Let no drunkard approach prayer. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, was called and it was recited to him, and he said: O Allah, clarify to us concerning wine a healing clarification. The verse in al-Maida came down, and Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, was called and it was recited to him, and when it reached: Will you then desist, Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: We desist, we desist."
"379","Affan narrated to us, Shuba narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Abu Wail, from Subayy ibn Mabad, that he was a Taghlibi Christian and became Muslim, and asked which deed is best, and it was said to him: Jihad in the way of Allah, Mighty and Majestic. He wanted to fight, and it was said to him: Have you performed hajj. He said: No. It was said to him: Perform hajj and umra, then fight. So he entered ihram for both together, and met Zayd ibn Suhan and Salman ibn Rabia, and they said: He is more astray than his she-camel, or he is no better guided than his camel. He went to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and told him what they said, and he said: You were guided to the sunna of your Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, or to the sunna of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"380","Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Hisham, he said: My father informed me that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to the Stone: You are only a stone, and were it not that I saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, kiss you, I would not kiss you. Then he kissed it."
"381","Waki narrated to us, from Hisham, from his father, that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, came to the Stone and said: I know you are a stone that neither harms nor benefits, and were it not that I saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, kiss you, I would not kiss you. He said: Then he kissed it."
"382","Waki narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Abd al-Ala, from Suwayd ibn Ghafala, that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, kissed it and embraced it, then said: I saw Abu al-Qasim, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, tender with you, meaning the Stone."
"383","Waki narrated to us, Hisham ibn Urwa narrated to us, from his father, from Asim ibn Umar, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: When night comes from here and day goes from here, the fasting person has broken his fast."
"384","Waki narrated to us, Hisham ibn Sad narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The likeness of one who takes back his charity is like one who returns to his vomit."
"385","Waki narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Maymun, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The people of Jahiliyya did not depart from Jam until they said: Shine, Thabir, that we may hasten. When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came, he opposed them, and used to depart from Jam by the measure of the prayer of travelers at the dawn prayer, before sunrise."
"386","Waki narrated to us, Rabah ibn Abi Maruf narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Mulayka, he heard Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, say: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to me: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: The dead is punished by the weeping of his family over him."
"387","Waki narrated to us, from Hasan ibn Salih, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, wipe over his khuffs while traveling."
"388","Waki narrated to us, from Israil, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Maymun, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to seek refuge from miserliness and cowardice and the punishment of the grave and the worst of age and the trial of the breast. Waki said: The trial of the breast is that a man dies, and Waki mentioned the trial without repenting from it."
"389","Waki narrated to us, Umar ibn al-Walid al-Shanni narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Burayda, he said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, sat in a sitting in which the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to sit, with funerals passing by him. He said: They passed with a funeral and praised good, and he said: It is due. Then they passed with a funeral and praised good, and he said: It is due. Then they passed with a funeral and they said good, and he said: It is due. Then they passed with a funeral and they said: This was the most lying of people. He said: The most lying of people are those who lie most against Allah, then those next to them, who lie about a soul in a body. He said: They said: What if four testify. He said: It is due. They said: Or three. He said: And three, it is due. They said: And two. He said: It is due. To have said one is dearer to me than red camels. He said: It was said to Umar: Is this something you say by your opinion, or something you heard from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. He said: No, I heard it from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"390","Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from his father, from Abaya ibn Rifaa, he said: It reached Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that Sad, when he built the palace, said: The sound is cut off. He sent to him Muhammad ibn Maslama, and when he came he brought out his fire-steel and kindled fire and bought firewood for a dirham, and it was said to Sad: A man did such and such. He said: That is Muhammad ibn Maslama. He went out to him and swore by Allah he did not say it. He said: We convey from you what you say, and do what we are ordered. He burned the door, then came offering to provision him, and he refused, and he left and came to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and hastened to him, going and returning nineteen. He said: Were it not good opinion of you, we would think you did not convey from us. He said: Yes, he sends greetings of peace and excuses and swears by Allah he did not say it. He said: Did he provision you anything. He said: No. He said: What prevented me from provisioning you myself. He said: I disliked to order for you and have the cool for you and the hot for me, while around me are the people of Medina hunger has killed them. I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: A man does not eat his fill while his neighbor is without. End of the Musnad of Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him. The hadith of al-Saqifa."
"391","Ishaq ibn Isa al-Tabba narrated to us, Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, Ibn Shihab narrated to me, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba ibn Masud, that Ibn Abbas informed him that Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf returned to his camp. Ibn Abbas said: I used to teach Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, and he found me waiting for him, at Mina in the last hajj Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, performed. Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf said: A man came to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: So-and-so says: If Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, dies, I will pledge to so-and-so. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I will stand this evening among the people and warn them of these men who want to usurp their affair. Abd al-Rahman said: I said: O Commander of the Faithful, do not do it, for the season gathers the riffraff and rabble of the people, and they overcrowd your gathering when you stand among the people, and I fear you will say a statement those will carry off without understanding it nor placing it in its places. But wait until you come to Medina, for it is the abode of hijra and sunna, and you will be alone with the scholars and nobles of the people, and say what you said with deliberation, and they will understand your statement and place it in its places. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: If I come to Medina safe and sound, I will speak of it to the people in the first station I stand. When we came to Medina after Dhu al-Hijja, and it was Friday, I hastened to go early at the blind mans rush. I said to Malik: What is the blind mans rush. He said: He does not care at what hour he goes out, not knowing heat and cold, and the like. I found Said ibn Zayd at the right corner of the minbar ahead of me, and I sat facing him, my knee touching his knee, and hardly had Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, come out when I saw him and said: He will say this evening upon this minbar a statement none before him said upon it. He said: Said ibn Zayd disapproved of that and said: What could he say that none said. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, sat upon the minbar, and when the muadhdhin fell silent, he stood and praised Allah as He deserves, then said: Now, O people, I will say a statement decreed for me to say, I do not know, perhaps before my term. Whoever understands and comprehends it, let him narrate it wherever his mount stops with him. Whoever does not understand it, I do not permit him to lie upon me. Allah, Blessed and Almighty, sent Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, with the truth and sent down upon him the Book, and among what was sent down upon him was the verse of stoning. We recited it and remembered it, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, stoned, and we stoned after him. I fear if time lengthens for the people that someone will say: We do not find the verse of stoning in the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and they will go astray by leaving an obligation Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent down. Stoning in the Book of Allah is true upon whoever commits zina, if muhsan, of men and women, when proof stands, or pregnancy, or confession. We used to recite: Do not turn away from your fathers, for it is disbelief in you to turn away from your fathers. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Do not extol me as Isa son of Maryam, upon him peace, was extolled, for I am only the servant of Allah, so say: The servant of Allah and His Messenger. It reached me that one of you says: If Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, dies, I will pledge to so-and-so. Let no man be deceived to say: The pledge of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, was a sudden slip. It was so, yet Allah, Mighty and Majestic, protected its evil. There is not among you today one to whom necks are cut like Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him. When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, died, Ali and al-Zubayr and those with them stayed back in the house of Fatima, may Allah be pleased with her, daughter of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and the Ansar altogether stayed back in the Saqifa of Banu Saida, and the Muhajirun gathered to Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him. I said to him: O Abu Bakr, let us go to our brothers of the Ansar. We set out toward them until two righteous men met us and told us what the people did, and said: Where are you going, O company of Muhajirun. I said: We want our brothers here of the Ansar. They said: You need not approach them, decide your affair, O company of Muhajirun. I said: By Allah, we will come to them. We went until we came to them in the Saqifa of Banu Saida, and they were gathered, and among them a man wrapped. I said: Who is this. They said: Sad ibn Ubada. I said: What is with him. They said: Ill. When we sat, their preacher stood and praised Allah, Mighty and Majestic, as He deserves, and said: Now, we are the helpers of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and the battalion of Islam, and you, O company of Muhajirun, are a group of us, and a party of you have come pressing, wanting to cut us from our root and push us from the affair. When he fell silent, I wanted to speak, and I had prepared a speech that pleased me, wanting to say it before Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, though I was wary of some of his sharpness, and he was more forbearing and dignified than me. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Gently. I disliked to anger him, and he was more knowledgeable and dignified than me. By Allah, he left no word that pleased me in my preparation but said it impromptu, and better, until he fell silent. He said: Now, what good you mentioned, you deserve it. The Arabs know this affair only for this tribe of Quraysh. They are the most central of the Arabs in lineage and abode. I approve for you one of these two men, whichever you wish. He took my hand and the hand of Abu Ubayda ibn al-Jarrah. I disliked nothing of what he said but that. By Allah, to be brought forward and have my neck struck, bringing me no closer to sin, was dearer to me than to rule over a people among whom is Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, unless my soul changes at death. One of the Ansar said: I am its rubbed stump and its propped cluster, from us an amir and from you an amir, O company of Quraysh. I said to Malik: What is meant by: I am its rubbed stump and its propped cluster. He said: As if saying: I am its calamity. He said: Clamor increased and voices rose until I feared disagreement, and I said: Stretch your hand, O Abu Bakr. He stretched his hand, and I pledged to him, and the Muhajirun pledged to him, then the Ansar pledged to him, and we rushed upon Sad ibn Ubada. One of them said: You killed Sad. I said: Allah killed Sad. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: By Allah, we found in what we attended no matter stronger than the pledge of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him. We feared if we left the people with no pledge, they would make a pledge after us, and we would either follow them in what we dislike, or oppose them with corruption in it. Whoever pledges to an amir without consultation of the Muslims, there is no pledge for him, nor for the one who pledged to him, lest they be killed. Malik said: Ibn Shihab informed me, from Urwa ibn al-Zubayr, that the two men they met were Uwaymir ibn Saida and Man ibn Adi. Ibn Shihab said: Said ibn al-Musayyab informed me that the one who said: I am its rubbed stump and its propped cluster was al-Hubab ibn al-Mundhir."
"392","Ishaq ibn Isa narrated to us, Malik informed me, from Yahya ibn Said, that he heard Anas ibn Malik say: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Shall I not inform you of the best quarters of the Ansar, Banu al-Najjar, then Banu Abd al-Ashhal, then Balharith ibn al-Khazraj, then Banu Saida. And he said: In all the quarters of the Ansar there is good."
"393","Ishaq ibn Isa narrated to us, Malik narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The two traders have the choice as long as they have not parted, or the sale is with choice."
"394","Ishaq ibn Isa narrated to us, Malik informed us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade the sale of habal al-habala."
"395","Ishaq ibn Isa narrated to us, Malik informed us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We used to trade food at the time of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he would send to us one who ordered us to move it from the place we bought it in to another place before we sold it."
"396","Ishaq ibn Isa narrated to us, Malik informed us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever buys food, let him not sell it until he takes full possession of it."
"397","Ishaq ibn Isa narrated to us, Malik informed us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever frees a share of his in a slave, and he has what reaches the price of the slave, it is valued a just value, and his partners are given their right, and the slave is freed upon him. Otherwise he has freed what he freed."
"398","Sufyan narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Said, he said: I said to Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him: A man invoked lian against his wife. He said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, separated between them, and he mentioned the hadith."
